Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:02.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK good afternoon, everybody.\N好的，下午好，大家。
Dialogue: 0,0:00:02.94,0:00:06.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,Thanks for coming.\N感谢各位的到来。
Dialogue: 0,0:00:06.36,0:00:14.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is the first of the first colloquium of the colloquium of the semester.\N这是本学期第一场系列讲座的第一场。
Dialogue: 0,0:00:14.12,0:00:15.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so welcome everybody.\N所以欢迎各位。
Dialogue: 0,0:00:15.58,0:00:24.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,We are here every Wednesday at this time, and we get quite a variety of topics and speakers.\N我们每周三的这个时间都会在这里举行，主题和演讲嘉宾种类繁多。
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.88,0:00:29.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,Generally we get very, very prominent high quality speakers.\N通常我们邀请的都是非常著名且高质量的演讲嘉宾。
Dialogue: 0,0:00:29.24,0:00:31.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,And today we start out with.\N今天我们将从。
Dialogue: 0,0:00:34.84,0:00:39.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,A very, very good first case.\N一个非常出色的开场案例开始。
Dialogue: 0,0:00:39.73,0:00:42.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,We actually have two speakers.\N我们今天有两位演讲嘉宾。
Dialogue: 0,0:00:42.47,0:00:44.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,We have Tony yen.\N有安东尼·颜。
Dialogue: 0,0:00:44.79,0:00:48.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,He's the vice president head of technology development at ASML.\N他是阿斯麦技术开发副总裁兼负责人。
Dialogue: 0,0:00:48.69,0:00:57.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,I assume all of you who are here have heard of ASML and know the key role they play in the semiconductor industry.\N我想在座各位应该都听说过阿斯麦，也了解他们在半导体行业中的关键作用。
Dialogue: 0,0:00:57.43,0:01:12.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,And Tony is currently in addition to his continuing in his role as VP and Head of technology development at ASML, he is also serving as a visiting professor in our department.\N此外，托尼除了继续担任阿斯麦的副总裁兼技术发展负责人外，还担任我们部门的访问教授。
Dialogue: 0,0:01:12.11,0:01:17.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,He will be teaching a course in the.\N他将开设一门课程。
Dialogue: 0,0:01:17.27,0:01:19.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,In the fall.\N秋季学期。
Dialogue: 0,0:01:19.15,0:01:22.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,Sorry spring semester on the topic of today's talk.\N抱歉，是春季学期，主题为今天讲座的内容。
Dialogue: 0,0:01:22.89,0:01:28.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,So if you're interested in this material, sign up for Tony's class.\N如果您对这些内容感兴趣，请报名参加托尼的课程。
Dialogue: 0,0:01:28.25,0:01:36.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Tony was he got his bachelor's degree from Purdue, his PhD and MBA from MIT.\N托尼本科毕业于普渡大学，博士和工商管理硕士均毕业于麻省理工学院。
Dialogue: 0,0:01:36.11,0:01:44.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,He was. He worked at TI and at TSMC for quite a while before joining ASML.\N他在加入阿斯麦之前，曾在德州仪器和台积电工作了很长时间。
Dialogue: 0,0:01:44.01,0:01:49.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,He's a fellow of IEEE and won a number of important awards.\N他是电气与电子工程师学会会士，并获得过多项重要奖项。
Dialogue: 0,0:01:49.57,0:01:54.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,Tony's going to start out today, but he has a colleague here, Ron Gosens,\N托尼今天将首先发言，但他还有一位同事在这里，罗纳德·古森斯，
Dialogue: 0,0:01:54.71,0:02:06.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,also Gosens, also from ASML, who is a senior strategy manager at ASML.\N也是来自阿斯麦的古森斯，担任阿斯麦的高级战略经理。
Dialogue: 0,0:02:06.03,0:02:07.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,He's working part time.\N他目前是兼职工作。
Dialogue: 0,0:02:07.43,0:02:10.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,He's partly retired.\N他部分退休。
Dialogue: 0,0:02:10.53,0:02:15.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,He is also an adjunct professor at Purdue, where he teaches,\N他还在普渡大学担任兼职教授，讲授课程。
Dialogue: 0,0:02:15.69,0:02:25.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,and he got educated in the Netherlands, at Utrecht and has been at Philips research, Stanford national, NXP\N他在荷兰乌得勒支接受教育，曾就职于飞利浦研究、斯坦福国家实验室和恩智浦。
Dialogue: 0,0:02:25.13,0:02:30.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,before joining ASML, where he leads computational lithography efforts.\N在加入阿斯麦之前，他负责计算光刻方面的研究工作。
Dialogue: 0,0:02:30.09,0:02:40.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,So they're going to do a tag team and tell us about EUV lithography, which is the hot thing now in microelectronics.\N他们将联手为我们介绍当前微电子领域的热门技术——极紫外光刻。
Dialogue: 0,0:02:40.30,0:02:43.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,And it'll give us the history and where we are and where we're going.\N这将为我们讲述其发展历程、当前状态以及未来方向。
Dialogue: 0,0:02:43.50,0:02:46.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I'll start with Tony.\N那么我先从安东尼开始。
Dialogue: 0,0:02:49.52,0:02:51.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,Thank you.\N谢谢。
Dialogue: 0,0:02:51.72,0:02:54.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,Thank you, Jeff, for this very nice introduction.\N谢谢杰夫，感谢您如此精彩的介绍。
Dialogue: 0,0:02:54.10,0:02:55.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,Is the microphone on.\N麦克风打开了吗？
Dialogue: 0,0:02:55.48,0:02:56.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yes thank you.\N是的，谢谢。
Dialogue: 0,0:02:56.72,0:03:03.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,And Ronald and I were very happy to be here at Berkeley to give this colloquium.\N罗纳德和我非常高兴能来到伯克利参加这次研讨会。
Dialogue: 0,0:03:03.62,0:03:08.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,And we without further ado, let me get started.\N那么，不耽误时间，我开始吧。
Dialogue: 0,0:03:08.88,0:03:10.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,Perhaps I'll get a little excited.\N也许我会有点激动。
Dialogue: 0,0:03:10.86,0:03:13.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let me remove my jacket first, just in case.\N那我先脱掉外套，以防万一。
Dialogue: 0,0:03:13.85,0:03:16.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,Don't think you want to stand in the middle because you'll walk.\N别站在中间，你会走动的。
Dialogue: 0,0:03:16.52,0:03:19.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,No, no, I will be standing here.\N不，不，我会站在这里。
Dialogue: 0,0:03:19.24,0:03:26.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we want to tell you about EUV lithography.\N所以我想跟你们讲讲极紫外光刻技术。
Dialogue: 0,0:03:26.68,0:03:30.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,But I want to start with some history.\N但我想先从一些历史讲起。
Dialogue: 0,0:03:30.68,0:03:34.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,But I'd like to start with the history of photolithography itself.\N但我想先讲讲光刻技术本身的历史。
Dialogue: 0,0:03:34.04,0:03:35.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,Just give me five minutes.\N给我五分钟时间。
Dialogue: 0,0:03:35.72,0:03:46.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because this may be interesting to know if some of you do not know photolithography or are not familiar with this technology yet, but.\N因为如果你们中有些人还不了解光刻技术，或者对这项技术还不熟悉，那这些信息可能就很有意思了。
Dialogue: 0,0:03:46.84,0:03:55.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let me get started, it's the first part of the talk is the photolithography until early 1980s and then EUV as a next\N那我开始讲了，这部分是讲光刻技术直到上世纪80年代初，\N然后是下一代光刻技术——极紫外光刻技术，以及如何开发极紫外光刻技术以实现大规模生产。
Dialogue: 0,0:03:55.58,0:04:03.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,generation technology, lithography technology, and then developing EUV lithography for high volume production.\N所以光刻技术本身是在20世纪50年代中期由两组人发明的。
Dialogue: 0,0:04:03.78,0:04:11.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,So photolithography itself was invented in the mid 1950s by two groups.\N所以光刻技术本身是在20世纪50年代中期由两组人发明的。
Dialogue: 0,0:04:11.26,0:04:15.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,First was called Bell Labs where you folks were.\N第一个是贝尔实验室，你们就在那里。
Dialogue: 0,0:04:15.46,0:04:18.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the second group was a US Army's laboratory.\N第二个小组是美国陆军的实验室。
Dialogue: 0,0:04:18.90,0:04:24.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let me focus on the second group because the story was more dramatic.\N所以我来重点讲第二个小组，因为这个故事更加戏剧化。
Dialogue: 0,0:04:24.86,0:04:32.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,In the second group, the goal was to fit a proximity fuse diffuse into a mortar shell.\N在第二个小组中，目标是将一个近炸引信装进迫击炮弹壳里。
Dialogue: 0,0:04:32.58,0:04:34.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,Mortar shells are not very big.\N迫击炮弹壳并不大。
Dialogue: 0,0:04:34.20,0:04:37.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,A proximity fuse is basically a cheap radar system.\N近炸引信本质上是一个廉价的雷达系统。
Dialogue: 0,0:04:37.88,0:04:46.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, the goal is to have the bomb explode in the proximity of the target before hitting the target.\N好的，目标是在炸弹击中目标之前，就在目标附近引爆。
Dialogue: 0,0:04:46.40,0:04:49.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,To increase the probability of impact.\N以提高命中概率。
Dialogue: 0,0:04:49.72,0:04:53.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because if it explodes right on target, you may miss the target.\N因为如果在击中目标的瞬间爆炸，可能会错过目标。
Dialogue: 0,0:04:53.62,0:04:57.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,But if you explore the vicinity and your chances are a lot higher hitting the target.\N但如果在目标周围引爆，命中目标的几率要高得多。
Dialogue: 0,0:04:57.98,0:05:02.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this was the goal to put the proximity fuse inside a mortar shell.\N所以目标就是把近炸引信放进迫击炮弹壳里。
Dialogue: 0,0:05:02.66,0:05:04.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK mortar shell is very small.\N好的，迫击炮弹壳非常小。
Dialogue: 0,0:05:04.60,0:05:10.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,So in those days the transistors were mesa transistors.\N当时的晶体管是台面晶体管。
Dialogue: 0,0:05:10.14,0:05:11.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,Germanium OK.\N锗材料可以。
Dialogue: 0,0:05:11.70,0:05:17.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,How do you fit a mesa germanium transistor circuit inside a mortar shell.\N如何把台面锗晶体管电路装进炮弹壳里？
Dialogue: 0,0:05:17.34,0:05:18.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, these two guys.\N就是这两个人。
Dialogue: 0,0:05:21.52,0:05:26.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,Jay Lathrop and James Knoll from the laboratory,\N实验室的杰伊·拉思罗普和詹姆斯·诺尔。
Dialogue: 0,0:05:26.18,0:05:37.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,they thought of using, of course, they found out that Kodak had a photoresist and they thought of using a microscope, but operating in reverse.\N他们想到，当然，他们发现柯达有一种光刻胶，于是想到用显微镜，但反过来操作。
Dialogue: 0,0:05:37.55,0:05:39.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this is a microscope.\N这是一台显微镜。
Dialogue: 0,0:05:39.73,0:05:43.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is where the transistor or a piece of germanium is.\N晶体管或一块锗材料就在这里。
Dialogue: 0,0:05:43.43,0:05:47.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,And they put the photomask here where you would have the Polaroid camera.\N他们把光刻掩模放在原本放宝丽来相机的位置。
Dialogue: 0,0:05:47.69,0:05:50.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you shine white light from the top.\N从上方照射白光。
Dialogue: 0,0:05:50.87,0:05:52.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now what is this filter.\N那个滤光片是什么？
Dialogue: 0,0:05:52.41,0:06:01.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, before the exposure, when you're aligning to the previous layer, you slide the red filter in there so that the photoresist is not exposed.\N在曝光前，对齐前一层时，他们会把红色滤光片滑进去，以防止光刻胶被曝光。
Dialogue: 0,0:06:01.15,0:06:06.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Once you do the alignment, you take out the red filter and then the exposure happens.\N完成对齐后，你移除红色滤镜，然后进行曝光。
Dialogue: 0,0:06:06.11,0:06:10.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,You expose this and then you get the transistor.\N你对这个进行曝光，之后就得到了晶体管。
Dialogue: 0,0:06:10.15,0:06:12.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the transistor looked like this.\N而这个晶体管看起来是这样的。
Dialogue: 0,0:06:12.25,0:06:16.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,The die is about 1 millimeter to 1 millimeter by 1 millimeter.\N芯片尺寸约为1毫米乘1毫米乘1毫米。
Dialogue: 0,0:06:16.97,0:06:17.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is a mesa.\N这是一个台面结构。
Dialogue: 0,0:06:17.97,0:06:19.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,The base size was not stated.\N基区尺寸未说明。
Dialogue: 0,0:06:19.57,0:06:20.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,The base contact.\N基区接触。
Dialogue: 0,0:06:20.87,0:06:28.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,This little guy, one of these guys is a base contact is 0.1 by 0.25.\N这个小部分，其中一个基区接触的尺寸是0.1乘0.25。
Dialogue: 0,0:06:28.43,0:06:30.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Nowadays it's gigantic.\N如今这已经非常巨大了。
Dialogue: 0,0:06:30.11,0:06:32.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,But back in those days, it was hard to make.\N但在当时，制造起来非常困难。
Dialogue: 0,0:06:32.17,0:06:37.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then they also had to make the emitter contact, which is the other rectangle right here.\N然后他们还需要制作发射区接触，也就是这里的另一个矩形。
Dialogue: 0,0:06:37.27,0:06:39.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,Same Same dimension.\N尺寸相同。
Dialogue: 0,0:06:39.69,0:06:41.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,It was a success.\N这是一次成功。
Dialogue: 0,0:06:41.33,0:06:47.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then what happened was that Jay Lathrop joined ti in 1958.\N然后发生的事情是，杰·拉思罗普在1958年加入了德州仪器。
Dialogue: 0,0:06:47.29,0:06:49.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,And what happened in TI at 19.\N德州仪器在1959年发生了什么。
Dialogue: 0,0:06:49.63,0:06:54.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,In 1958, Jack Kilby invented the integrated circuit.\N1958年，杰克·基尔比发明了集成电路。
Dialogue: 0,0:06:54.39,0:07:00.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,So Jay Lathrop worked at TI with Jack Kilby for the next 10 years.\N于是杰·拉思罗普在接下来的十年里与杰克·基尔比在德州仪器共事。
Dialogue: 0,0:07:00.53,0:07:03.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,And where did the other guy go.\N那个其他人去了哪里。
Dialogue: 0,0:07:03.65,0:07:06.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,Of course, he went to Fairchild.\N当然，他去了仙童公司。
Dialogue: 0,0:07:06.21,0:07:14.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,1959 right where Robert Noyce and others had invented the integrated circuit there.\N1959年，就在罗伯特·诺伊斯和其他人发明集成电路的地方。
Dialogue: 0,0:07:14.61,0:07:18.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,So great story, and it's a bit romantic.\N所以这是一个精彩的故事，也带点浪漫色彩。
Dialogue: 0,0:07:18.85,0:07:22.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this was in the mid to late 1950s.\N这发生在20世纪50年代中期到晚期。
Dialogue: 0,0:07:22.69,0:07:24.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,And what happened next.\N接下来发生了什么。
Dialogue: 0,0:07:24.65,0:07:29.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,What happened next is less romantic lithography.\N接下来发生的事则没那么浪漫，是光刻技术。
Dialogue: 0,0:07:29.94,0:07:38.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,In the 60s and the 70, early 70s were mainly contact and proximity printing.\N20世纪60年代和70年代，早期主要是接触式和接近式光刻。
Dialogue: 0,0:07:38.44,0:07:43.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because those days, resolution wasn't really the top issue.\N那时候，分辨率并不是最核心的问题。
Dialogue: 0,0:07:43.86,0:07:46.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,The top issue was how to get these layers to align.\N最核心的问题是如何让这些层对准。
Dialogue: 0,0:07:46.86,0:07:50.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,So those machines were called aligners back then.\N所以那时候这些机器被称为对准仪。
Dialogue: 0,0:07:50.96,0:07:57.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is an aligner from Casper instruments here in the US.\N这是一台来自美国卡斯珀仪器公司的对准仪。
Dialogue: 0,0:07:57.52,0:08:02.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,These guys were the King back in the 60s OK.\N他们在20世纪60年代是行业霸主。
Dialogue: 0,0:08:02.20,0:08:05.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,They were very good at doing contact printing.\N他们在接触式光刻方面非常擅长。
Dialogue: 0,0:08:05.08,0:08:07.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,We put the mask right on top of the wafer.\N我们把掩模直接放在晶圆上方。
Dialogue: 0,0:08:07.30,0:08:08.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,You're aligned.\N你已经对准了。
Dialogue: 0,0:08:08.18,0:08:09.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then you shine the light.\N然后你照射光线。
Dialogue: 0,0:08:09.96,0:08:16.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,The problem with contact printing is that it generates particles in the space or.\N接触式光刻的问题在于，会在空间中产生颗粒。
Dialogue: 0,0:08:16.68,0:08:20.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, you're rubbing the wafer against the mask.\N你实际上是在让晶圆与掩模相互摩擦。
Dialogue: 0,0:08:20.12,0:08:22.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,So what happened was that later.\N后来发生的事情是这样。
Dialogue: 0,0:08:22.76,0:08:27.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, maybe we should create a gap between the mask and the wafer.\N或许我们应该在掩模和晶圆之间留出一个间隙。
Dialogue: 0,0:08:27.76,0:08:31.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then they went from contact printing to proximity printing.\N然后他们从接触式光刻转向了近距光刻。
Dialogue: 0,0:08:31.90,0:08:37.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,Once proximity printing happens, canon took over as the new King.\N近距光刻出现后，佳能成为了新的王者。
Dialogue: 0,0:08:37.62,0:08:39.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,This guy faded away.\N这个人逐渐淡出了。
Dialogue: 0,0:08:39.96,0:08:45.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then canon made a much better machine for proximity printing.\N然后佳能制造出了一台性能更出色的近距光刻机。
Dialogue: 0,0:08:45.90,0:08:50.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this is canon machine until.\N从那时起，佳能的机器一直占据主导地位。
Dialogue: 0,0:08:50.98,0:08:56.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, until proximity printing was superseded by projection imaging.\N直到近距光刻被投影成像技术取代。
Dialogue: 0,0:08:56.74,0:09:00.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this happened in the early 70s.\N这一转变发生在上世纪70年代初。
Dialogue: 0,0:09:00.22,0:09:03.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let me first give an example.\N让我先举一个例子。
Dialogue: 0,0:09:03.10,0:09:06.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,Why did we go for projection imaging.\N为什么我们要转向投影成像技术。
Dialogue: 0,0:09:06.06,0:09:17.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the example is let's shine a lambda equal to 400 nanometer UV light through a mask.\N举个例子，让我们用波长为400纳米的紫外光透过掩模。
Dialogue: 0,0:09:17.34,0:09:22.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,Imaging a slit slit is 4 micron wide slit right.\N一个4微米宽的狭缝，想象一下就是这样。
Dialogue: 0,0:09:22.12,0:09:27.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the gap is 1 micron 5 micron and 10 microns.\N而间隙分别是1微米、5微米和10微米。
Dialogue: 0,0:09:27.36,0:09:30.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is the kind of aerial image that you get.\N这就是你所得到的这种空中图像。
Dialogue: 0,0:09:30.62,0:09:39.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,As you can see that this is almost at the limit of resolution Of proximity printing.\N你可以看到，这几乎已经达到了邻近印刷分辨率的极限。
Dialogue: 0,0:09:39.08,0:09:39.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because why.\N因为原因在于。
Dialogue: 0,0:09:39.94,0:09:42.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because you really cannot control the gap very well.\N因为你真的无法很好地控制间隙。
Dialogue: 0,0:09:42.30,0:09:45.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,If you control the gap perfectly then it's fine.\N如果你能完美控制间隙，那就没问题。
Dialogue: 0,0:09:45.52,0:09:50.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,But you can't because first the control itself mechanism itself wasn't very good.\N但你做不到，首先是因为控制机制本身就不够好。
Dialogue: 0,0:09:50.70,0:09:55.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,But also the wafer has is not flat.\N而且晶圆本身也不平整。
Dialogue: 0,0:09:55.60,0:09:56.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's warped.\N它是弯曲的。
Dialogue: 0,0:09:56.68,0:10:01.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,So it's much better if you do it through a lens.\N所以通过透镜来实现要好得多。
Dialogue: 0,0:10:01.64,0:10:09.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is projection printing same wavelengths of light with a lens that is not very sophisticated.\N这就是投影印刷，使用相同波长的光，通过一个并不复杂的透镜。
Dialogue: 0,0:10:09.50,0:10:13.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,0.2 a very low numerical aperture lens.\N0.2，一个非常低的数值孔径镜头。
Dialogue: 0,0:10:13.00,0:10:15.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is what you get if you're unfocused.\N这就是你对焦不准时得到的结果。
Dialogue: 0,0:10:15.50,0:10:17.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,You're looking at the red curve.\N你看到的是红色曲线。
Dialogue: 0,0:10:17.02,0:10:23.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,And if you're off by plus 5 plus minus 5 microns you're looking at the blue curve.\N如果你偏离了正负5微米，你看到的就是蓝色曲线。
Dialogue: 0,0:10:23.39,0:10:28.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,So in one case you're fighting diffraction.\N在一种情况下，你在对抗衍射。
Dialogue: 0,0:10:28.59,0:10:32.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,The other case, you're taking advantage of diffraction.\N在另一种情况下，你在利用衍射。
Dialogue: 0,0:10:32.03,0:10:34.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,Of course, with the addition of a lens.\N当然，是加上了镜头之后。
Dialogue: 0,0:10:34.91,0:10:39.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this is what's going on in terms of physics.\N这就是其中的物理原理。
Dialogue: 0,0:10:39.43,0:10:49.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then of course, early 1970s, there was this company called perkin-elmer.\N然后当然，在20世纪70年代初，有一家公司叫珀金埃尔默。
Dialogue: 0,0:10:49.51,0:10:57.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,They made the first commercially successful projection aligner, to be precise, in 1973.\N他们于1973年制造出了第一台 commercially 成功的投影对准仪。
Dialogue: 0,0:10:57.91,0:11:05.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,This, this was the perkin-elmer microline 1X, meaning no magnification or magnification.\N这就是珀金埃尔默的 Microline 1X，意味着无放大或等倍放大。
Dialogue: 0,0:11:05.69,0:11:09.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,Reflective imaging system with an annular slit.\N带环形狭缝的反射成像系统。
Dialogue: 0,0:11:09.25,0:11:10.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's actually quite clever.\N其实非常巧妙。
Dialogue: 0,0:11:10.73,0:11:18.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,The designer of this optics was called Abe offner, a gigantic figure in lens design.\N这套光学系统的设计者名叫阿贝·奥弗纳，是镜头设计领域的一位巨擘。
Dialogue: 0,0:11:18.43,0:11:20.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,Sorry he passed away.\N很遗憾他已经去世了。
Dialogue: 0,0:11:20.53,0:11:24.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,But this system has only two mirrors.\N但这个系统只有两个镜片。
Dialogue: 0,0:11:24.31,0:11:29.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,Concentric concentric, two mirrors, one concave, one convex.\N同心同心，两个镜片，一个凹面，一个凸面。
Dialogue: 0,0:11:29.19,0:11:34.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the imaging is done through a slit y slit.\N成像是通过狭缝对狭缝实现的。
Dialogue: 0,0:11:34.31,0:11:45.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because Abe figured out that the optimal distance from the optical axis is this thing called H0 at H0 from the optical axis.\N因为阿贝发现，从光轴到最佳距离就是这个称为 H0 的位置。
Dialogue: 0,0:11:45.73,0:11:52.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,The aberration was minimized through a slit is fine because you can scan the whole thing,\N通过狭缝可以很好地消除像差，因为你能够扫描整个区域。
Dialogue: 0,0:11:52.59,0:12:00.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,because the mask and the wafer are actually ganged together through a shaft, which is not shown down here.\N因为掩模和晶圆实际上是通过一根轴连接在一起的，这一点在下面没有显示出来。
Dialogue: 0,0:12:00.11,0:12:03.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the exposure happens after alignment.\N所以曝光是在对准之后进行的。
Dialogue: 0,0:12:03.23,0:12:05.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,You basically do this right.\N你基本上只要正确操作这一步。
Dialogue: 0,0:12:05.23,0:12:09.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,You can never go wrong because they're together in one axis OK.\N你永远不会出错，因为它们在同一个轴上。
Dialogue: 0,0:12:09.65,0:12:13.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this was the first of course.\N当然，这是第一个。
Dialogue: 0,0:12:13.23,0:12:15.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,Then it became a hit.\N然后它成了热门产品。
Dialogue: 0,0:12:15.49,0:12:28.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,Thousands of these machines were sold and the first one went to Texas Instruments for a price of 90,000 $95,000.\N数千台这类机器被售出，第一台以9万到9万5千美元的价格卖给了德州仪器。
Dialogue: 0,0:12:28.60,0:12:32.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,It cannot even get a mask with this kind of money.\N用这笔钱甚至买不到一个光刻掩模。
Dialogue: 0,0:12:32.24,0:12:33.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,Euv mask, of course.\N当然是EUV掩模。
Dialogue: 0,0:12:33.72,0:12:36.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this was sold to TI.\N所以这个被卖给了德州仪器。
Dialogue: 0,0:12:36.12,0:12:37.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then what happened.\N然后发生了什么。
Dialogue: 0,0:12:37.84,0:12:39.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,Intel 8088.\N英特尔8088。
Dialogue: 0,0:12:39.92,0:12:41.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, this was not ti.\N好吧，这并不是德州仪器。
Dialogue: 0,0:12:41.14,0:12:42.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,This was Intel.\N这是英特尔。
Dialogue: 0,0:12:42.64,0:12:49.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,A device size was 0.2 by point is about 5 millimeters by 5 millimeter device size.\N器件尺寸为0.2毫米，约为5毫米乘5毫米。
Dialogue: 0,0:12:49.98,0:12:51.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,With contact printing.\N采用接触式曝光。
Dialogue: 0,0:12:51.14,0:13:01.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,The yield of these microprocessors were only 20% with micro microline 60% This is basically it.\N这些微处理器的良率只有20%，而微米线的良率是60%。基本上就是这样了。
Dialogue: 0,0:13:01.52,0:13:05.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean, Moore's law alive back then.\N我的意思是，那时候摩尔定律还活着。
Dialogue: 0,0:13:05.40,0:13:10.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,And now, of course, this wasn't the end of the story.\N而现在，当然，这并不是故事的终点。
Dialogue: 0,0:13:10.26,0:13:18.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,They were the King for a few years until the King is dead and long live the King.\N他们曾是王者几年，直到旧王已死，新王万岁。
Dialogue: 0,0:13:18.02,0:13:20.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,The new King, of course.\N当然，新王者。
Dialogue: 0,0:13:20.26,0:13:22.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,Who's the new King.\N新王者是谁？
Dialogue: 0,0:13:22.26,0:13:29.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, the problem with the one x design was that it was a simple optical system.\N一x设计的问题在于，它是一个简单的光学系统。
Dialogue: 0,0:13:29.90,0:13:32.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,Two mirrors, all spherical.\N两面镜子，都是球面的。
Dialogue: 0,0:13:32.08,0:13:33.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,Both spherical.\N两者都是球面的。
Dialogue: 0,0:13:33.90,0:13:37.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the could not be made very high.\N所以它们无法做到很高。
Dialogue: 0,0:13:37.26,0:13:42.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,The numerical aperture of the microline was 0.167.\N微米线的数值孔径是0.167。
Dialogue: 0,0:13:42.04,0:13:42.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,Very low.\N非常低。
Dialogue: 0,0:13:42.98,0:13:48.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,In order to get better resolution, you need to increase the numerical aperture.\N为了获得更高的分辨率，你需要增大数值孔径。
Dialogue: 0,0:13:48.26,0:13:54.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,However, in order to do so on the offner system, the aberration would become very high.\N但在这种光刻系统上实现这一点时，像差会变得非常高。
Dialogue: 0,0:13:54.46,0:14:07.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we had to get away from this kind of a offner design and to come back to the more traditional D magnified lens design.\N因此，我们必须摆脱这种光刻系统设计，转而采用更传统的D倍放大镜头设计。
Dialogue: 0,0:14:07.16,0:14:12.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is what happened in the late 70s, early 80s.\N这正是20世纪70年代末、80年代初发生的情况。
Dialogue: 0,0:14:12.06,0:14:14.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,The first champion was GCA.\N第一个领先者是GCA。
Dialogue: 0,0:14:14.38,0:14:20.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK GCA made a G Line system called a 4,800 man.\N好的，GCA推出了一款名为4,800的G线光刻系统。
Dialogue: 0,0:14:20.33,0:14:28.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,And now I have to say a little bit about the resolution in projection lithography.\N现在，我需要简单谈一下投影光刻中的分辨率。
Dialogue: 0,0:14:28.99,0:14:30.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,Only projection lithography.\N仅限于投影光刻。
Dialogue: 0,0:14:30.99,0:14:32.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Approximately lithography.\N大致是光刻技术。
Dialogue: 0,0:14:32.11,0:14:33.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,The resolution formula is different.\N分辨率公式有所不同。
Dialogue: 0,0:14:33.89,0:14:39.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is K1 lambda over and this is minimum half pitch.\N这是K1乘以λ，再除以最小半间距。
Dialogue: 0,0:14:39.37,0:14:42.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,We're just talking about this with Eli.\N我们刚才正和伊莱讨论这个问题。
Dialogue: 0,0:14:42.89,0:14:49.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,Minimum half pitch right is a more accurate metric in our sense.\N最小半间距在我们的衡量标准下是一个更准确的指标。
Dialogue: 0,0:14:49.91,0:14:56.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because if you want to compare all the systems, you don't want to compare with, oh, how thin a line you can print.\N因为你想要比较所有系统时，不希望拿“你能打印的线条有多细”来做对比。
Dialogue: 0,0:14:56.23,0:14:57.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's always minimum half pitch.\N始终是最低的半间距。
Dialogue: 0,0:14:57.91,0:15:05.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because in minimum with minimum half pitch this K1 has a lower limit, which Ronald will talk about 0.25.\N因为在最小半间距下，K1 有一个下限，罗纳德将会提到这个值是 0.25。
Dialogue: 0,0:15:05.73,0:15:11.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then the minimum pitch is lambda over can decrease lambda or you can increase the numerical aperture.\N然后最小间距可以降低，方法是减小波长 λ，或者提高数值孔径。
Dialogue: 0,0:15:11.67,0:15:22.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,Numerical aperture is the sign of this half angle at times n n n index of the medium surrounding this lens.\N数值孔径是这个半角的正弦值乘以透镜周围介质的折射率 n。
Dialogue: 0,0:15:22.69,0:15:31.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,Of course, this end is always one until you got to immersion lithography right at 193, then n became 1.44.\N当然，这个值通常为 1，直到进入 193 纳米浸没式光刻，此时 n 变为 1.44。
Dialogue: 0,0:15:31.47,0:15:33.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, so this is what happened.\N好，这就是发生的情况。
Dialogue: 0,0:15:33.57,0:15:43.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then of course later k RF lithography with excimer laser reliable excimer laser became available.\N然后，当然，后来出现了使用准分子激光的 KrF 光刻，可靠的准分子激光器得以实现。
Dialogue: 0,0:15:43.03,0:15:53.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then, of course, after that was the ARF excimer laser at 193 and immersion 193 with water 193.\N之后，便是 193 纳米的 ArF 准分子激光，以及使用水的 193 纳米浸没式光刻。
Dialogue: 0,0:15:53.41,0:15:57.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the effective wavelength became 144 OK.\N因此有效波长变为 144，明白了吗？
Dialogue: 0,0:15:57.07,0:16:01.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then of course, we have EUV lithography.\N然后，当然，我们有了 EUV 光刻。
Dialogue: 0,0:16:01.23,0:16:06.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Euv lithography took a long time to develop.\NEUV 光刻技术的开发花了很长时间。
Dialogue: 0,0:16:06.11,0:16:14.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,Why Because in the early days, we did not think we could optical lithography could go beyond this.\N因为在早期，我们并不认为光学光刻能够突破这一极限。
Dialogue: 0,0:16:14.33,0:16:17.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,Why exactly this thing.\N正好是因为这个原因。
Dialogue: 0,0:16:17.03,0:16:22.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is called a point spread function because is cut off here.\N这被称为点扩散函数，因为在这里被截断了。
Dialogue: 0,0:16:22.35,0:16:31.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,So if you have a point source on a mask, point object on a mask, you never get a point here.\N所以如果你在掩模上有一个点光源，即掩模上的一个点物体，你在这里永远得不到一个点。
Dialogue: 0,0:16:31.43,0:16:40.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,You get a diffraction limited point spread function, which is this spot which is 1.2 to lambda over.\N你得到的是一个受衍射限制的点扩散函数，也就是这个光斑，其大小约为 1.2 倍的 λ 除以数值孔径。
Dialogue: 0,0:16:40.09,0:16:47.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now, you can say that, OK, I can increase the numerical aperture and I get a smaller spot.\N现在你可能会说，好吧，我可以增加数值孔径，从而得到更小的光斑。
Dialogue: 0,0:16:47.59,0:16:50.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,But no, not if you have lots of aberrations.\N但如果没有消除大量像差，就做不到。
Dialogue: 0,0:16:50.47,0:16:54.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,If you increase the numerical aperture then the spot won't get any smaller.\N如果你增加数值孔径，光斑也不会变得更小。
Dialogue: 0,0:16:54.67,0:17:05.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is the early days, exactly what we were facing, because we did not know how to make high Na optics without inducing a lot of aberration.\N这正是我们早期所面临的问题，因为我们不知道如何制造高数值孔径光学系统，而又不引入大量像差。
Dialogue: 0,0:17:05.65,0:17:08.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, when we were here.\N好，当我们处于这个阶段时。
Dialogue: 0,0:17:08.21,0:17:16.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is when people started to work on NGL, next generation lithography technologies, and EUV was one of them.\N这正是人们开始着手研发下一代光刻技术的时候，而 EUV 就是其中之一。
Dialogue: 0,0:17:16.80,0:17:25.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, so EUV development happened in the mid 1980s, and it went into high volume production in 2019.\N好的，EUV 的研发始于20世纪80年代中期，2019年进入大规模生产。
Dialogue: 0,0:17:25.82,0:17:28.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,35 years of development.\N35年的研发历程。
Dialogue: 0,0:17:28.76,0:17:32.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, so let me now get into EUV lithography.\N好的，现在我来讲解一下EUV光刻技术。
Dialogue: 0,0:17:32.52,0:17:38.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,The first paper on EUV lithography in the US happened in 1988.\N美国第一篇关于EUV光刻的论文发表于1988年。
Dialogue: 0,0:17:38.68,0:17:48.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,In 1987, a guy called Andy Herrlich, working at Lawrence Livermore laboratory back then was a laboratory that belonged to the University of California here.\N1987年，一位名叫安迪·赫里利希的科学家当时在劳伦斯利弗莫尔实验室工作，该实验室隶属于加州大学。
Dialogue: 0,0:17:48.80,0:17:59.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,And he thought that they had perfected by then the multilayer mirrors to reflect soft X-ray light.\N他认为当时他们已经完善了多层镜面，能够反射软X射线。
Dialogue: 0,0:17:59.90,0:18:01.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,Back then, EUV wasn't the name.\N当时EUV还不是一个名称。
Dialogue: 0,0:18:01.58,0:18:08.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,Soft X-ray was the name for about the kind of wavelength that's today EUV.\N软X射线是当时对如今EUV波长范围的称呼。
Dialogue: 0,0:18:08.36,0:18:17.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,And he published this paper, of course, he said he proposed to use a reflecting mask instead of a transmission mask because reflecting mass.\N他发表了这篇论文，当然，他提出使用反射掩模而非透射掩模，因为反射掩模。
Dialogue: 0,0:18:17.84,0:18:20.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,The mask substrate is also multilayered.\N掩模基板本身也是多层结构。
Dialogue: 0,0:18:20.12,0:18:24.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,Any proposal to use laser produced plasma.\N任何关于使用激光产生等离子体的提议。
Dialogue: 0,0:18:24.22,0:18:28.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,Both of these concepts are adopted nowadays.\N这两个概念如今都被采用。
Dialogue: 0,0:18:28.54,0:18:37.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,And of course, 1989 he and Nat saglio and others actually again they went to the three beams meeting\N当然，1989年，他和纳特·萨尔廖以及其他人再次前往三束交汇处
Dialogue: 0,0:18:37.78,0:18:44.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,and they showed a mask that they made multilayer mask with gold absorber pattern.\N展示了他们制作的多层掩模，采用金吸收层图案
Dialogue: 0,0:18:44.44,0:18:48.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,Of course, we don't use gold, but multilayers were used.\N当然，我们不用金，但多层结构确实被使用了
Dialogue: 0,0:18:48.20,0:18:49.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,And here you go.\N看这里
Dialogue: 0,0:18:49.96,0:18:52.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,See the wavelength is about 13 nanometers.\N你看，波长约为13纳米
Dialogue: 0,0:18:52.36,0:18:54.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,Today we use 13.5.\N如今我们使用的是13.5纳米
Dialogue: 0,0:18:54.98,0:18:59.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this was actually the first paper in 1988, in the US.\N这实际上是1988年在美国发表的第一篇论文
Dialogue: 0,0:18:59.38,0:19:07.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,But in 1989, there was also a paper from a Japanese guy called hiroo Kinoshita.\N但1989年，一位名叫木下博夫的日本学者也发表了一篇论文
Dialogue: 0,0:19:07.38,0:19:13.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,Kinoshita published a paper look at this soft X-ray reduction in lithography using multilayer mirrors.\N木下发表了一篇论文，展示了利用多层镜片实现软X射线光刻的减小
Dialogue: 0,0:19:13.55,0:19:14.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,Very direct.\N非常直接
Dialogue: 0,0:19:14.65,0:19:19.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,And he shows this is his setup a Schwarzschild optics with a reflecting mask.\N他展示了自己搭建的装置，采用施瓦茨希尔德光学系统和反射式掩模
Dialogue: 0,0:19:19.81,0:19:28.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,And these were the results 0.5 micron lines and spaces from a micron line spaces mass.\N这些是实验结果：从1微米线宽的掩模中获得了0.5微米的线宽和间距
Dialogue: 0,0:19:28.79,0:19:32.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,So it's an 8x reduction camera.\N所以这是一台8倍缩小的相机。
Dialogue: 0,0:19:32.29,0:19:34.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,The wavelength used was 12.4.\N使用的波长是12.4。
Dialogue: 0,0:19:34.75,0:19:38.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,So he could not have used Morley's PSI multilayers.\N所以他不可能使用了莫利的PSI多层膜。
Dialogue: 0,0:19:38.35,0:19:46.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's a different multilayer because this is just below the absorption edge of silicon at 12.5.\N这是不同的多层膜，因为这个波长仅略低于硅的吸收边，即12.5。
Dialogue: 0,0:19:46.25,0:19:48.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,But this wasn't his first paper.\N但这并不是他的第一篇论文。
Dialogue: 0,0:19:48.87,0:19:53.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,His first paper was actually three years before, in 1986,\N他的第一篇论文实际上早了三年，发表于1986年，
Dialogue: 0,0:19:53.55,0:20:02.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,published in Japanese in Japan at the Japan applied Physics Society meeting for meeting 1986.\N在日本应用物理学会1986年会议上以日文发表。
Dialogue: 0,0:20:02.29,0:20:03.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,This was a.\N这是。
Dialogue: 0,0:20:03.31,0:20:08.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,A, the results were much cruder, but this was the first,\N结果粗糙得多，但这是第一篇，
Dialogue: 0,0:20:08.27,0:20:18.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,so we normally regard him as the inventor of EUV lithography, although in the US Andy Hurley was the first one.\N所以我们通常认为他是极紫外光刻技术的发明者，尽管在美国，安迪·赫利是第一个。
Dialogue: 0,0:20:18.31,0:20:26.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, Andy actually told me that he did not know the results of Kinoshita, which I believe this was published entirely\N好的，安迪本人告诉我，他并不知道木下研究的结果，我相信这是因为当时这些成果完全
Dialogue: 0,0:20:26.71,0:20:34.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,in Japanese back in those days, it wasn't translated into English, but Kinoshita San was the first one.\N以日文发表，尚未翻译成英文，但木下先生是第一个。
Dialogue: 0,0:20:34.21,0:20:44.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,But there was a third group, the Bell Labs folks, the Bell Labs folks, two people, originally Albert wood and Bill sylvest.\N但还有第三组人，就是贝尔实验室的人，贝尔实验室的两个人，最初是阿尔伯特·伍德和比尔·西尔维斯特。
Dialogue: 0,0:20:44.11,0:20:50.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,They were working on a 37.5 sodium laser.\N他们当时正在研究一种波长为37.5纳米的钠激光。
Dialogue: 0,0:20:50.87,0:20:57.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,They thought that, hey, we could make this to do photolithography, such a small wavelength.\N他们认为，嘿，我们可以用这种波长极短的激光来做光刻。
Dialogue: 0,0:20:57.67,0:21:02.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,And they actually proposed at first the offner type of optics.\N他们最初提出使用奥弗纳型光学系统。
Dialogue: 0,0:21:02.79,0:21:08.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,They want to coat the offner optics with multilayers and then use that to do lithography.\N他们想给奥弗纳光学系统镀多层膜，然后用它来进行光刻。
Dialogue: 0,0:21:08.45,0:21:15.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, for some reason, that wasn't what the Bell Labs subsequently did.\N但不知什么原因，贝尔实验室后来并没有采用这种方法。
Dialogue: 0,0:21:15.53,0:21:24.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,Subsequently, the results came from not the after optics, but the Schwarzschild optics.\N后来，成果并非来自奥弗纳光学系统，而是来自施瓦茨希尔德光学系统。
Dialogue: 0,0:21:24.61,0:21:27.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,And Jeff Bowker right here.\N而杰夫·鲍克就在这里。
Dialogue: 0,0:21:27.27,0:21:28.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,The second name.\N第二个名字。
Dialogue: 0,0:21:28.11,0:21:31.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you were heavily involved in this.\N你深度参与了这项工作。
Dialogue: 0,0:21:31.29,0:21:35.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this paper was published in 1990.\N这篇论文发表于1990年。
Dialogue: 0,0:21:35.29,0:21:41.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,And Schwarzschild optics at a wavelength of 14, very close to today's wavelengths.\N施瓦茨希尔德光学系统在14纳米波长下表现优异，非常接近当今的波长。
Dialogue: 0,0:21:41.55,0:21:43.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,But they use the transmission mask.\N但他们使用了传输掩模。
Dialogue: 0,0:21:43.85,0:21:47.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is a membrane mask, not the multilayer mask.\N这是一种膜掩模，而不是多层掩模。
Dialogue: 0,0:21:47.89,0:21:49.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,But look at the results.\N但看看结果。
Dialogue: 0,0:21:49.71,0:21:51.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,Look at the results.\N看看结果。
Dialogue: 0,0:21:51.69,0:21:54.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,Pitch equal to 100 nanometer.\N线距等于100纳米。
Dialogue: 0,0:21:54.37,0:21:57.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,Back in those days, those were the best results.\N当时，这些是最佳结果。
Dialogue: 0,0:21:57.59,0:21:58.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,I was a student.\N我当时还是个学生。
Dialogue: 0,0:21:58.63,0:22:02.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,I said, wow, my advisor Hank Smith tried to downplay this.\N我说，哇，我的导师汉克·史密斯试图淡化这一点。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:02.45,0:22:02.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,Oh yeah.\N哦，对。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:02.95,0:22:03.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, yeah.\N对，对。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:03.19,0:22:04.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,But they got a lot of aberrations.\N但他们遇到了很多像差。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:04.83,0:22:05.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,But it doesn't matter.\N但没关系。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:05.75,0:22:06.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,It doesn't matter.\N这并不重要。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:06.50,0:22:08.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because to do this, they had to open up.\N因为要做到这一点，他们必须打开光圈。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:08.86,0:22:12.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is normally is 0.08.\N通常这个数值是 0.08。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:12.32,0:22:18.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,0.08 if you put in the formula you couldn't get this because this is on axis illumination.\N如果你用公式计算，无法得到这个结果，因为这是轴向照明。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:18.00,0:22:20.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the smallest K1 is 0.5.\N所以最小的 k1 值是 0.5。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:20.90,0:22:26.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,So in order to get this they open up this aperture to 0.16 OK.\N为了达到这个效果，他们将光圈打开到 0.16，没问题。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:26.10,0:22:30.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,In order to get that of course that introduces a lot of aberrations but that's OK.\N为了实现这一点，当然会引入大量像差，但这没关系。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:30.64,0:22:33.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,They published this OK.\N他们发表了这项成果。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:33.08,0:22:35.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,Those were the best results.\N这是当时最好的结果。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:35.12,0:22:43.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then in the mid 90s, Intel led by Intel, something called the chief technology guy at Intel.\N然后在 90 年代中期，英特尔由英特尔的首席技术官牵头。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:43.14,0:22:52.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,Those days, they formed this thing called the EUV, LLC and EUV, LLC was formed.\N当时，他们成立了名为 EUV, LLC 的组织，EUV, LLC 由此成立。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:52.14,0:22:56.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then members joined and those members were competitors of Intel.\N然后成员加入，这些成员是英特尔的竞争对手。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:56.60,0:22:57.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,Nonetheless, they joined.\N尽管如此，他们还是加入了。
Dialogue: 0,0:22:57.86,0:23:05.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,They bought shares of EUV, LLC at $5 million per share AMD, IBM, Infinium micro model.\N他们以每股500万美元的价格购买了EUV, LLC的股份，\N包括AMD、IBM和Infinium微模型。
Dialogue: 0,0:23:05.98,0:23:09.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,They are joined and SEMATECH joined,\N他们加入了，而SEMATECH也加入了。
Dialogue: 0,0:23:09.16,0:23:22.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,and then the money was pulled and farmed out to virtual National Laboratory Lawrence Livermore, Sandia and Lawrence Berkeley headed by Dave Attwood.\N然后资金被撤回，并分配给了虚拟国家实验室——劳伦斯利弗莫尔、\N桑迪亚以及劳伦斯伯克利实验室，由戴夫·阿特伍德领导。
Dialogue: 0,0:23:22.04,0:23:29.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,Here And this was the time when ASML joined EUV, LLC.\N这正是阿斯麦加入EUV, LLC的时候。
Dialogue: 0,0:23:29.62,0:23:32.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,Euv, LLC was critical.\NEUV, LLC至关重要。
Dialogue: 0,0:23:32.00,0:23:37.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,Before EUV, LLC, you might say, OK, we have disparate groups.\N在EUV, LLC成立之前，你可以说，我们只是分散的团体。
Dialogue: 0,0:23:37.28,0:23:38.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,They were gangsters.\N他们是一群恶棍。
Dialogue: 0,0:23:38.44,0:23:40.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is organized crime.\N这是有组织的犯罪。
Dialogue: 0,0:23:40.90,0:23:46.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,And a lot of IP a lot of good work.\N但其中包含了大量知识产权和大量优秀的工作成果。
Dialogue: 0,0:23:46.06,0:23:51.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,But EUV LLC ramped down in 2002, 2003.\N然而，EUV, LLC在2002年、2003年逐渐缩减规模。
Dialogue: 0,0:23:51.66,0:23:58.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,However, when EUV LLC ramped down, ASML ramped up in 2006,\N但当EUV, LLC缩减规模时，阿斯麦在2006年却开始加速发展。
Dialogue: 0,0:23:58.80,0:24:03.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,we shipped first two EUV machines, these called ADT alpha.\N我们交付了前两台EUV设备，它们被称为ADT alpha。
Dialogue: 0,0:24:03.55,0:24:07.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,Demo tools one went to Albany.\N其中一台设备送到了阿尔巴尼。
Dialogue: 0,0:24:07.05,0:24:10.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,The other one went to our partner IMEC.\N另一台送到了我们的合作伙伴imec。
Dialogue: 0,0:24:10.27,0:24:14.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this picture was taken April of 2007.\N这张照片拍摄于2007年4月。
Dialogue: 0,0:24:14.21,0:24:19.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,Two was accepted in a year later of course.\N这两台设备一年后才被正式接受。
Dialogue: 0,0:24:19.21,0:24:22.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,Took a long time to have this tool installed and debugged.\N这台设备的安装和调试花费了很长时间。
Dialogue: 0,0:24:22.61,0:24:24.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,And it used a discharge.\N它使用了放电技术。
Dialogue: 0,0:24:24.51,0:24:28.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,Produced a plasma source which we no longer use nowadays.\N产生了等离子体光源，但我们现在已不再使用。
Dialogue: 0,0:24:28.53,0:24:31.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,So these were the first two tools.\N这就是最初的两台设备。
Dialogue: 0,0:24:31.25,0:24:33.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,The pictures are from the second tool.\N这些照片来自第二台设备。
Dialogue: 0,0:24:33.07,0:24:34.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,At IMEC.\N在imec。
Dialogue: 0,0:24:34.33,0:24:37.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,I don't have a picture of the Albany tool.\N我没有阿尔巴尼那台设备的照片。
Dialogue: 0,0:24:37.41,0:24:44.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then from that point onward we developed 6,100.\N从那时起，我们开发了6100型设备。
Dialogue: 0,0:24:44.83,0:24:48.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,These are the next generation beyond the ADT.\N这是ADT之后的下一代设备。
Dialogue: 0,0:24:48.97,0:24:53.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,Six of them were developed and went to various companies.\N其中有六台被开发出来，并分发给了不同的公司。
Dialogue: 0,0:24:53.25,0:24:58.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,And my former company TSMC got the number 6 tool.\N我之前所在的公司台积电获得了第6台设备。
Dialogue: 0,0:24:58.17,0:25:01.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,The number 6 tool came to Taiwan.\N第6台设备来到了台湾。
Dialogue: 0,0:25:01.31,0:25:06.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,This picture was taken by me on the tarmac of taoyuan International Airport.\N这张照片是我拍摄于桃园国际机场的停机坪上。
Dialogue: 0,0:25:06.41,0:25:08.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,Summer of 1911.\N1911年的夏天。
Dialogue: 0,0:25:08.19,0:25:15.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then four months later, this was almost installed in our fab fab 12.\N四个月后，这台设备几乎就安装在我们的12厂里了。
Dialogue: 0,0:25:15.47,0:25:19.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,Inside TSMC shinshu R&D fab.\N在台积电新竹研发中心的晶圆厂内。
Dialogue: 0,0:25:19.23,0:25:25.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,And now how good could we do with this 0.25 micron R&D tool.\N现在我们用这台0.25微米的研发设备，能做到什么程度？
Dialogue: 0,0:25:25.91,0:25:35.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,This was a photo of the lowest metal layer with a pitch of 46 nanometers.\N这是金属层最底层的照片，节距为46纳米。
Dialogue: 0,0:25:35.79,0:25:39.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is after hard mask etched through right.\N这是在硬掩模蚀刻完成后拍摄的。
Dialogue: 0,0:25:39.79,0:25:48.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,This was intended to be the design of 10 nanometer generation, but it did not happen.\N这原本是10纳米世代的设计，但最终未能实现。
Dialogue: 0,0:25:48.83,0:25:49.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,Look at this.\N看这个。
Dialogue: 0,0:25:49.37,0:25:51.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is a two dimensional design.\N这是一个二维设计。
Dialogue: 0,0:25:51.05,0:25:55.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,In the end, the 10 nanometer was a 1D design because EUV could not make it.\N最终，10纳米工艺变成了1D设计，因为EUV无法实现。
Dialogue: 0,0:25:55.89,0:26:01.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,And TSMC we had to continue with immersion lithography.\N而台积电，我们不得不继续使用浸没式光刻。
Dialogue: 0,0:26:01.57,0:26:09.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we changed it to a self-aligned etch process and a 1D design.\N所以我们改为了自对准刻蚀工艺和1D设计。
Dialogue: 0,0:26:09.21,0:26:13.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,But had EUV been ready, we would have continued with the 2D design.\N但如果EUV已经就绪，我们本会继续采用二维设计。
Dialogue: 0,0:26:13.75,0:26:15.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, that's another story.\N好，这是另一个故事。
Dialogue: 0,0:26:15.73,0:26:18.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now I want to just give you a sneak preview.\N现在我想先给你们一个提前预览。
Dialogue: 0,0:26:18.27,0:26:25.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,11 years later, we could do this with the high-na EUV machine.\N11年后，我们借助高数值孔径EUV设备实现了这一点。
Dialogue: 0,0:26:25.05,0:26:26.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,Again, a 2D design.\N再次，一个二维设计。
Dialogue: 0,0:26:26.93,0:26:29.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now is 0.55.\N现在是0.55。
Dialogue: 0,0:26:29.27,0:26:32.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is from our first machine.\N这是我们的第一台机器。
Dialogue: 0,0:26:32.01,0:26:33.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,You can see the pitch.\N你可以看到间距。
Dialogue: 0,0:26:33.97,0:26:35.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,The pitch was 46.\N间距是46。
Dialogue: 0,0:26:35.39,0:26:38.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,The pitch is now 22.\N现在间距是22。
Dialogue: 0,0:26:38.41,0:26:39.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,So that's progress.\N所以这是进步。
Dialogue: 0,0:26:39.83,0:26:42.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,Again, this is etched into a hard mask.\N再次强调，这是蚀刻在硬掩模上的。
Dialogue: 0,0:26:42.81,0:26:43.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,All right.\N好的。
Dialogue: 0,0:26:43.55,0:26:47.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the EUV development at TSMC.\N台积电的极紫外光刻技术发展。
Dialogue: 0,0:26:47.73,0:26:51.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is a chronology of EUV development at TSMC.\N这是台积电极紫外光刻技术发展的年表。
Dialogue: 0,0:26:51.97,0:27:02.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,Not only did we work with ASML very closely and tested EUV on 10 nanometer, 7 nanometer, 5 nanometer test\N我们不仅与阿斯麦紧密合作，并在10纳米、7纳米、5纳米测试层上进行了极紫外光刻测试，
Dialogue: 0,0:27:02.76,0:27:09.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,layers. But we also had to participate in infrastructure development.\N还必须参与基础设施建设。
Dialogue: 0,0:27:09.92,0:27:12.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,Imec has always been a member.\Nimec 一直都是其中一员。
Dialogue: 0,0:27:12.20,0:27:18.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,We rejoined the SEMATECH Berkeley Lab, of course sorry, the Metz.\N我们重新加入了伯克利实验室的SEMATECH，当然抱歉，是默茨。
Dialogue: 0,0:27:18.44,0:27:31.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,And we also joined the adac in Japan to develop the help develop the actinic blank inspector and also the co-funding of EUV development with ASML to ASML along with Intel and Samsung.\N我们还加入了日本的ADAC，以协助开发活体空白检测仪，\N并与阿斯麦、英特尔和三星共同资助极紫外光刻技术的研发。
Dialogue: 0,0:27:31.80,0:27:34.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,This was led by me of course.\N这个项目当然由我主导。
Dialogue: 0,0:27:34.98,0:27:40.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,Then in 2017 I left and joined ASML.\N2017年，我离开后加入了阿斯麦。
Dialogue: 0,0:27:40.04,0:27:44.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,In 2019, this is when EUV went into high volume production.\N2019年，极紫外光刻技术进入大规模生产。
Dialogue: 0,0:27:44.96,0:27:56.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,As you can see that this was a paper published by TSMC at IDM 2019, where they announced that the 7 nanometer plus node has\N如你所见，这是台积电在2019年IDM大会上发表的一篇论文，他们宣布7纳米及以上节点已
Dialogue: 0,0:27:56.56,0:28:02.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,had been developed and used layers of EUV lithography.\N采用多层极紫外光刻技术进行开发和应用。
Dialogue: 0,0:28:02.32,0:28:09.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,And of course, they also announced that the next year that the 5 nanometer node will have more EUV layers.\N当然，他们还宣布，次年5纳米节点将采用更多极紫外光刻层。
Dialogue: 0,0:28:09.30,0:28:12.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this is the first part of the story.\N这是故事的第一部分。
Dialogue: 0,0:28:12.20,0:28:17.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK from the beginning to high volume manufacturing in 2019.\N好的，从最初到2019年实现大规模制造。
Dialogue: 0,0:28:17.92,0:28:20.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let me move on to the second part.\N那么让我进入第二部分。
Dialogue: 0,0:28:20.54,0:28:22.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK last slide.\N好的，最后一张幻灯片。
Dialogue: 0,0:28:22.14,0:28:27.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is what ASML did right 222 alpha demo tools in 2006.\N这就是阿斯麦在2006年成功完成222 alpha演示工具的原因。
Dialogue: 0,0:28:27.22,0:28:31.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,And a bunch of six of them R&D tools in 2010.\N然后在2010年，又推出了一批六台研发工具。
Dialogue: 0,0:28:31.92,0:28:33.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,Starting in 2010.\N从2010年开始。
Dialogue: 0,0:28:33.24,0:28:42.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,But in 2013 ASML did the third generation 3,300 B 3300b lens design is the same as the 3,100, but the Germans thought that\N但到了2013年，阿斯麦推出了第三代产品，3300b的镜头设计与3100相同，但德国人认为
Dialogue: 0,0:28:42.94,0:28:49.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,with better fabrication, they could enlarge the DNA from 0.25 to 0.33.\N通过更先进的制造工艺，他们可以将数值孔径从0.25提升至0.33。
Dialogue: 0,0:28:49.62,0:28:53.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is the Na that we're using until now.\N这就是我们至今仍在使用的数值孔径。
Dialogue: 0,0:28:53.90,0:28:55.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,All the subsequent machines.\N后续所有机器都沿用了这一设计。
Dialogue: 0,0:28:55.95,0:28:59.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,0.33 machines all use the same lens design.\N所有0.33数值孔径的机器均采用相同的镜头设计。
Dialogue: 0,0:28:59.15,0:29:05.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,Sorry and then in 2019, we have the 3400c the workhorse.\N抱歉，然后在2019年，我们推出了3400c，也就是主力机型。
Dialogue: 0,0:29:05.61,0:29:09.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then all these machines are being retired.\N之后这些机器陆续退役。
Dialogue: 0,0:29:09.55,0:29:12.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,But 3400c still working.\N但3400c仍在正常运行。
Dialogue: 0,0:29:12.11,0:29:16.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then to 2021 we came out with 3600d.\N到2021年，我们推出了3600d。
Dialogue: 0,0:29:16.21,0:29:19.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now that was the first part.\N现在这是第一部分。
Dialogue: 0,0:29:19.35,0:29:21.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,Second part is the current status.\N第二部分是当前状态。
Dialogue: 0,0:29:21.69,0:29:25.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,First the 0.33 Na, which is the workhorse.\N首先是0.33 NA，这是主力机型。
Dialogue: 0,0:29:25.01,0:29:29.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then the 0.55 the next generation high Na.\N然后是0.55 NA，下一代高数值孔径机型。
Dialogue: 0,0:29:29.05,0:29:34.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this is our latest 0.33 machine.\N这是我们最新的0.33 NA机型。
Dialogue: 0,0:29:34.19,0:29:36.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,The 3,800 right.\N3800，没错。
Dialogue: 0,0:29:36.03,0:29:38.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,We've already gone through 3,000 403,600.\N我们已经完成了3000片403600的生产。
Dialogue: 0,0:29:38.67,0:29:40.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,And now the 3,800.\N现在是3800。
Dialogue: 0,0:29:40.15,0:29:44.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,The 3,800 machine is quite a nice machine.\N3800机型非常出色。
Dialogue: 0,0:29:44.36,0:29:51.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,And of course, the throughput has been increased to 220 wafers per hour at 30 millijoules per centimeter square.\N当然，产能已提升至每小时220片晶圆，能量密度为每平方厘米30毫焦。
Dialogue: 0,0:29:51.35,0:29:58.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,You always have to specify the dose if the dose is very high, of course, the throughput comes down.\N你总是需要指定剂量，如果剂量过高，产能自然会下降。
Dialogue: 0,0:29:58.03,0:30:07.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,We have a 500 Watt EUV source now and then we have a very low aberration projection optics here.\N我们现在配备了一台500瓦的EUV光源，同时这里还采用了极低像差的投影光学系统。
Dialogue: 0,0:30:07.71,0:30:11.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is the 3,800.\N这是 3,800。
Dialogue: 0,0:30:11.61,0:30:13.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,And how did it do.\N那么它的表现如何？
Dialogue: 0,0:30:13.05,0:30:17.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well first, let me show you some overlay number.\N首先，让我展示一些套刻数据。
Dialogue: 0,0:30:17.47,0:30:26.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is a dedicated chalk overlay meaning that the wafer gets on one Chuck and you expose it and it gets on the same Chuck,\N这是一种专用的套刻测试，意味着晶圆被放置在一个卡盘上进行曝光，然后仍放在同一个卡盘上再次曝光，
Dialogue: 0,0:30:26.29,0:30:29.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,and you expose it again, and you measure the overlay.\N再测量套刻精度。
Dialogue: 0,0:30:29.13,0:30:36.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this dedicated overlay number is extremely good, 0.50.6 nanometer.\N这种专用套刻数据非常出色，达到 0.5 到 0.6 纳米。
Dialogue: 0,0:30:36.51,0:30:38.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'm not talking about one point.\N我说的不是单一点的数据。
Dialogue: 0,0:30:38.61,0:30:46.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Each field has 49 points 76 fields per wafer times 12 wafers.\N每个区域有 49 个测量点，每片晶圆有 76 个区域，共 12 片晶圆。
Dialogue: 0,0:30:46.11,0:30:50.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,These numbers came from these many points.\N这些数据来自如此多的测量点。
Dialogue: 0,0:30:50.89,0:30:52.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is not just a 3 sigma.\N这不仅仅是 3 西格玛。
Dialogue: 0,0:30:52.74,0:30:54.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's mean plus minus 3 sigma.\N而是均值加减 3 西格玛。
Dialogue: 0,0:30:54.88,0:30:56.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is the whole thing.\N这就是全部数据。
Dialogue: 0,0:30:56.70,0:30:59.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,And match machine overlay.\N并匹配机器上的套刻。
Dialogue: 0,0:30:59.82,0:31:04.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,Meaning that overlay read on two different machines.\N意味着在两台不同机器上读取的套刻。
Dialogue: 0,0:31:04.04,0:31:05.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,This one is not working.\N这个不行。
Dialogue: 0,0:31:05.96,0:31:11.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's OK on two different machines, but of the same type of both 3,800.\N在两台不同机器上都没问题，但都是同类型的 3,800。
Dialogue: 0,0:31:11.80,0:31:14.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then we get 0.9 and 0.8.\N然后我们得到 0.9 和 0.8。
Dialogue: 0,0:31:14.80,0:31:16.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,Still very good.\N仍然非常好。
Dialogue: 0,0:31:16.14,0:31:17.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK still very good.\N好的，仍然非常好。
Dialogue: 0,0:31:17.98,0:31:23.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you can see that we were below the spec OK.\N你可以看到我们低于规格，没问题。
Dialogue: 0,0:31:23.58,0:31:25.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now what's the reliability.\N那么可靠性如何？
Dialogue: 0,0:31:25.84,0:31:27.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean, are they all always down.\N我的意思是，它们总是都宕机吗？
Dialogue: 0,0:31:27.72,0:31:29.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,No, no they're mostly up.\N不，不，它们大部分时间都是运行的。
Dialogue: 0,0:31:29.50,0:31:35.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,The availability is 93.5% worldwide on all the machines.\N所有机器在全球范围内的可用性为 93.5%。
Dialogue: 0,0:31:35.46,0:31:36.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is the average availability.\N这是平均可用性。
Dialogue: 0,0:31:36.84,0:31:38.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,So it's already very good.\N所以它已经非常好。
Dialogue: 0,0:31:38.32,0:31:39.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,Not as good as the deep-v machines.\N还不如深紫外机器。
Dialogue: 0,0:31:39.92,0:31:40.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,Deep-v machines.\N深紫外机器。
Dialogue: 0,0:31:40.60,0:31:41.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,They are.\N它们确实如此。
Dialogue: 0,0:31:41.18,0:31:48.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,The availability is above 95% So yes, we continue to work on it to improve the availability.\N可用性高于95%，所以是的，我们仍在继续努力，以进一步提高可用性。
Dialogue: 0,0:31:48.28,0:31:52.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,And how many of these machines are there.\N这些机器有多少台？
Dialogue: 0,0:31:52.00,0:31:54.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,Here's the cumulative number.\N这是累计数量。
Dialogue: 0,0:31:54.20,0:32:02.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,By the end of Q4 2024, about 270 machines are in the field all running OK.\N到2024年第四季度末，大约有270台机器已在现场运行，一切正常。
Dialogue: 0,0:32:02.84,0:32:05.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,34 603,800.\N34603800。
Dialogue: 0,0:32:05.18,0:32:16.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,These three types, the 3,300 have been retired about 270 machines by the end of last year, all working either in R&D or production.\N这三种型号中，3300型已退役，到去年年底共约270台仍在运行，分别用于研发或生产。
Dialogue: 0,0:32:16.44,0:32:20.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now, how many wafers can you produce a day.\N现在，你一天能生产多少片晶圆？
Dialogue: 0,0:32:20.34,0:32:23.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,All these questions are asked by our customers.\N所有这些问题都是我们的客户提出的。
Dialogue: 0,0:32:23.16,0:32:31.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,The number of wafers that we produce today on average is about 3,000 on a single system.\N我们目前在单个系统上平均生产的晶圆数量约为3000片。
Dialogue: 0,0:32:31.76,0:32:33.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,We can do a little better.\N我们还能做得更好一些。
Dialogue: 0,0:32:33.38,0:32:37.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,So it's between 3,000 and 3,500.\N所以这个数字在3000到3500之间。
Dialogue: 0,0:32:37.72,0:32:41.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,Of course, we're going to continue to improve this number.\N当然，我们会继续提升这个数值。
Dialogue: 0,0:32:41.84,0:32:42.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,All right.\N好的。
Dialogue: 0,0:32:42.80,0:32:45.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,So where are we going from here.\N那么接下来我们该往哪里走？
Dialogue: 0,0:32:45.26,0:32:51.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, of course scaling has slowed down but it has not stopped.\N当然，缩放速度已经放缓，但并未停止。
Dialogue: 0,0:32:51.38,0:32:54.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,So scaling will continue.\N因此缩放将继续进行。
Dialogue: 0,0:32:54.06,0:33:01.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,And because of that, we started to OK, this is another thing.\N正因为如此，我们开始考虑，好吧，这是另一件事。
Dialogue: 0,0:33:01.10,0:33:06.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,We're not scaling as steeply as before.\N我们的缩放幅度没有以前那么陡峭了。
Dialogue: 0,0:33:06.22,0:33:13.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,This slope, this was the slope down to 3 nanometer generation.\N这个斜率，就是通往3纳米工艺节点的斜率。
Dialogue: 0,0:33:13.70,0:33:16.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you can see an inflection point, a kink.\N你可以看到一个拐点，一个转折。
Dialogue: 0,0:33:16.46,0:33:18.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,Starting from 3 nanometer.\N从3纳米开始。
Dialogue: 0,0:33:18.66,0:33:20.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,It comes down more gradually.\N它下降得更加平缓。
Dialogue: 0,0:33:20.68,0:33:24.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,We're never going to go back to this kind of slope.\N我们永远不会再回到这种斜率。
Dialogue: 0,0:33:24.30,0:33:27.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,However we have not stopped OK.\N但我们的进展并未停止。
Dialogue: 0,0:33:27.44,0:33:32.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,This was really a turning point at 3:00 nanometer generation.\N这确实是3纳米世代的一个关键转折点。
Dialogue: 0,0:33:32.46,0:33:46.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because of that, we 10 years ago, we started to develop a hyena system 0.55 because there is a need for scaling.\N正因为如此，10年前我们就开始研发Hyena系统0.55，因为存在扩展的需求。
Dialogue: 0,0:33:46.30,0:33:50.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,Good that good thing that the slope has not stopped.\N好在斜率没有停止。
Dialogue: 0,0:33:50.25,0:33:54.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, so system design for years.\N好，那么系统设计持续了多年。
Dialogue: 0,0:33:54.13,0:33:57.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,Module manufacturing started in 2018.\N模块制造始于2018年。
Dialogue: 0,0:33:57.89,0:34:01.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,In 2012 we integrated the modules.\N2012年我们完成了模块集成。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:01.05,0:34:01.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is the source.\N这是源头。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:01.95,0:34:04.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is if right here intermediate focus.\N这是中间焦点的位置。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:04.99,0:34:06.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,The scanner will be here.\N扫描仪将位于此处。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:06.49,0:34:08.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is the vessel.\N这是腔体。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:08.41,0:34:09.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,The vessel.\N腔体。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:09.73,0:34:17.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is the illuminator picture of the illuminator made by Zeiss.\N这是蔡司制造的照明器的图像。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:17.25,0:34:21.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's the wafer stage we make in veldhoven, Holland.\N这是我们在荷兰费尔德霍芬制造的晶圆台。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:21.33,0:34:23.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,The radical stage made here in the US.\N这是我们在美国制造的极紫外光刻机台。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:23.95,0:34:25.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,Connecticut Wilton, Connecticut.\N康涅狄格州威尔顿。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:25.89,0:34:33.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, so you put all these things together, and we put together the first machine in 2023, in veldhoven.\N好的，把这些部件组合起来后，我们在2023年于费尔德霍芬组装了第一台机器。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:33.85,0:34:36.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then in the.\N然后在。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:36.47,0:34:39.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then the next machine I'll show you in the next slide.\N接下来的机器我将在下一页幻灯片中展示。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:39.83,0:34:45.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,But then the next machine in veldhoven is the machine for the joint lab with IMEC.\N但接下来在费尔德霍芬的下一台机器，是与imec联合实验室的机器。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:45.13,0:34:50.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,But before this happened, we shipped a machine to our.\N但在那之前，我们已经向我们的客户发送了一台机器。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:50.47,0:34:53.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, first let me talk about the optics.\N好的，首先让我谈谈光学部分。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:53.43,0:34:55.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is the biggest piece.\N这是最大的一块。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:55.07,0:34:58.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is the largest mirror facing the wafer.\N这是面向晶圆的最大镜片。
Dialogue: 0,0:34:58.95,0:35:06.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,This mirror has a diameter of 1 meter and an aberration number of 20 picometers.\N这面镜子的直径为1米，像差为20皮米。
Dialogue: 0,0:35:06.11,0:35:09.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK yeah, that's actually shown here.\N对，这实际上就在这里展示了。
Dialogue: 0,0:35:09.59,0:35:19.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,The first machine actually went to Intel right before the end of 2023.\N第一台机器实际上是在2023年底之前交付给英特尔的。
Dialogue: 0,0:35:19.23,0:35:21.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,Of course, it takes a few.\N当然，需要几个。
Dialogue: 0,0:35:21.53,0:35:24.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,747 to ship the whole machine.\N用747运输整台机器。
Dialogue: 0,0:35:24.21,0:35:25.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is just one of them.\N这只是其中之一。
Dialogue: 0,0:35:25.99,0:35:32.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,And it was going into Intel's R&D fab in Portland, Oregon.\N它将进入位于俄勒冈州波特兰的英特尔研发晶圆厂。
Dialogue: 0,0:35:35.35,0:35:41.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,And wrapping cranes as you can see.\N而且像你们看到的那样，用吊车包裹起来。
Dialogue: 0,0:35:41.11,0:35:42.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,Quite heavy.\N非常重。
Dialogue: 0,0:35:46.42,0:35:48.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is the frame.\N这是框架。
Dialogue: 0,0:35:48.44,0:35:49.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is the illuminator.\N这是光源。
Dialogue: 0,0:35:49.44,0:35:54.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,Here OK yeah.\N这里可以，对吧。
Dialogue: 0,0:35:54.26,0:35:57.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, this video came from Intel, and this is the end of the video.\N这段视频来自英特尔，这是视频的结尾。
Dialogue: 0,0:35:57.92,0:36:01.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you can see that the wafer stage is not in there yet.\N你可以看到晶圆台还没有装进去。
Dialogue: 0,0:36:01.14,0:36:04.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,I don't know why they had this plastic thing to cover it up.\N我不知道他们为什么要用这个塑料罩盖住。
Dialogue: 0,0:36:04.06,0:36:05.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,The lens is already in there.\N镜头已经装进去了。
Dialogue: 0,0:36:05.76,0:36:08.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,The illuminator is not in there yet.\N光源还没有装进去。
Dialogue: 0,0:36:08.14,0:36:11.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the radical stage on top is not in there yet.\N而顶部的晶圆台也还没有装进去。
Dialogue: 0,0:36:11.20,0:36:13.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,But the source is already in there.\N但光源已经装进去了。
Dialogue: 0,0:36:13.42,0:36:17.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this picture tells a lot of story.\N所以这张照片讲了很多故事。
Dialogue: 0,0:36:17.06,0:36:23.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is inside Intel's R&D fab in Oregon.\N这是在英特尔俄勒冈州的研发晶圆厂内部。
Dialogue: 0,0:36:23.34,0:36:27.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then, of course, the next machine we put it in veldhoven.\N然后，当然，我们接下来把这台机器运到了飞利浦的费尔德霍芬。
Dialogue: 0,0:36:27.64,0:36:29.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is a joint lab with IMEC.\N这是一个与imec联合设立的实验室。
Dialogue: 0,0:36:29.52,0:36:39.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,Normally a machine would go to IMEC, but for the first machine, in order to maintain it more easily, we put it in veldhoven, smack in the middle of our factory.\N通常机器会直接送往imec，但第一台机器为了更方便维护，\N我们将其放在了费尔德霍芬，就在我们工厂的正中央。
Dialogue: 0,0:36:39.70,0:36:40.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,And let me see.\N让我看看。
Dialogue: 0,0:36:40.82,0:36:42.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,Get the animation started.\N开始播放动画。
Dialogue: 0,0:36:46.12,0:36:48.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, this is real.\N没错，这是真实的。
Dialogue: 0,0:36:48.36,0:36:51.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,The machine made it to a Tor track.\N这台机器已经抵达了托尔赛道。
Dialogue: 0,0:36:51.76,0:36:57.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,And there's a metrology room with all the ancillary stuff is located.\N旁边是测量室，所有辅助设备都集中在这里。
Dialogue: 0,0:36:57.58,0:37:00.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,So this is the high end lab we have at IMEC.\N这就是我们在imec拥有的高端实验室。
Dialogue: 0,0:37:00.40,0:37:05.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then the next few slides, I'll show results from this lab.\N接下来几张幻灯片，我将展示这个实验室的成果。
Dialogue: 0,0:37:05.68,0:37:09.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,First result is the DRAM, right.\N第一个结果是DRAM，没错。
Dialogue: 0,0:37:09.50,0:37:18.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,Drams the most critical two layers in DRAM are the hexagonal storage node lithography.\NDRAM中最关键的两层是六边形存储单元光刻。
Dialogue: 0,0:37:18.96,0:37:22.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now the state of the art is right here.\N当前最先进的技术就在这里。
Dialogue: 0,0:37:22.48,0:37:23.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's 34.\N它是34。
Dialogue: 0,0:37:23.68,0:37:31.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,This has just entered HVM high volume manufacturing.\N这项技术刚刚进入HVM高产量制造阶段。
Dialogue: 0,0:37:31.00,0:37:32.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,Leading edge DRAM makers.\N先进DRAM制造商。
Dialogue: 0,0:37:32.62,0:37:34.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,You can see with pitch 34.\N你可以看到节距为34。
Dialogue: 0,0:37:34.44,0:37:37.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,Low-high and low can still do it.\N低-高和低仍能做到这一点。
Dialogue: 0,0:37:37.78,0:37:39.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,In fact, it's done with low.\N事实上，它是用低工艺实现的。
Dialogue: 0,0:37:39.44,0:37:42.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,Nowadays in manufacturing OK.\N如今在制造上是可行的。
Dialogue: 0,0:37:42.70,0:37:46.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,But Hina would have done it with ease.\N但日亚（Hina）会轻松完成。
Dialogue: 0,0:37:46.22,0:37:55.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then, of course, when you go beyond page 34 Lo, it will become much more difficult.\N然后，当然，当你超过34低节距时，难度将大大增加。
Dialogue: 0,0:37:55.38,0:38:01.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,The The equivalent dose would be very high.\N其等效剂量会非常高。
Dialogue: 0,0:38:01.52,0:38:06.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,The reason is because you could not make use of the full illuminator.\N原因在于你无法充分利用整个照明系统。
Dialogue: 0,0:38:06.18,0:38:08.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,You lose light.\N会损失光线。
Dialogue: 0,0:38:08.30,0:38:12.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,So equivalently that means the dose is higher with high Na.\N因此等效地意味着，使用高数值孔径时剂量更高。
Dialogue: 0,0:38:12.08,0:38:12.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,No problem.\N没问题。
Dialogue: 0,0:38:12.84,0:38:15.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,You go on to pitch 28 and below.\N你接下来会进入 28 纳米及以下的节点。
Dialogue: 0,0:38:15.82,0:38:17.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is the other layer.\N这是另一层结构。
Dialogue: 0,0:38:17.98,0:38:19.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,There are two layers right.\N一共有两层，对吧。
Dialogue: 0,0:38:19.24,0:38:21.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,One storage node, which is a positive tone.\N一个存储单元，呈正性。
Dialogue: 0,0:38:21.32,0:38:26.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,And a negative tone is the storage node pad that sits underneath the storage node.\N而负性的是位于存储单元下方的存储节点接触垫。
Dialogue: 0,0:38:26.62,0:38:32.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,The capacitors again using low at pitch 32 are very difficult.\N再次强调，使用低至 32 纳米节距的电容非常困难。
Dialogue: 0,0:38:32.36,0:38:35.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,Very difficult. With this thing is more difficult.\N非常困难。而用这个结构则更难。
Dialogue: 0,0:38:35.72,0:38:44.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,The reason is that it has the periphery circuit, the subwoofer driver and the sense amplifiers in one shot with hayana.\N原因在于它将外围电路、低音炮驱动器和感测放大器整合在了一起，\N而这是在海亚纳（Hayana）技术下实现的。
Dialogue: 0,0:38:44.17,0:38:46.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,Very good. adi?\N很好，阿迪？
Dialogue: 0,0:38:46.25,0:38:52.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,After this is after H and at 28 after H. It looks good.\N这是在 H 之后的第 28 个位置，看起来不错。
Dialogue: 0,0:38:52.79,0:38:55.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,So hayana has got a lot of potential.\N所以，海亚纳具有很大的潜力。
Dialogue: 0,0:38:55.07,0:38:59.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's not in HVM yet, but we hope that it will be HVM soon.\N目前还不在 HVM 中，但我们希望它很快就能进入 HVM。
Dialogue: 0,0:38:59.97,0:39:01.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now this is logic, OK.\N现在这是逻辑层，明白了吗？
Dialogue: 0,0:39:01.91,0:39:03.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,Logic circuit.\N逻辑电路。
Dialogue: 0,0:39:03.19,0:39:06.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,The hardest layer to print is metal 0.\N最难打印的层是金属层 0。
Dialogue: 0,0:39:06.85,0:39:08.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's the lowest metal layer.\N这是最底层的金属层。
Dialogue: 0,0:39:08.51,0:39:16.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now it's printed with the low 0.33 with two or three multiple exposures.\N现在使用 0.33 的低数值孔径，通过两到三次多重曝光来打印。
Dialogue: 0,0:39:16.31,0:39:18.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,Purple is one where Brown is another one.\N紫色是其中一个，棕色是另一个。
Dialogue: 0,0:39:18.67,0:39:21.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then you have these cut layers.\N然后你还有这些通孔层。
Dialogue: 0,0:39:21.41,0:39:26.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,If you were to expose it with hayana you could do this in one shot.\N如果用海亚纳来曝光，你就可以一次完成。
Dialogue: 0,0:39:26.15,0:39:31.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,In the interest of time, I'm going to go through just to show you this result from IMEC.\N为了节省时间，我将快速展示一下 imec 的这项结果。
Dialogue: 0,0:39:31.33,0:39:34.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,A pitch equal to 19, which we're not there yet.\N线距为 19，我们目前还达不到这个水平。
Dialogue: 0,0:39:34.51,0:39:36.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK we're at pitch 23 right now.\N好的，我们现在线距是 23。
Dialogue: 0,0:39:36.51,0:39:41.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,At three nanometer node, pitch 19 will be future node note with a tip to tip.\N在三纳米节点上，线距 19 将是未来的节点，以尖对尖计算。
Dialogue: 0,0:39:41.49,0:39:45.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,The tip to tip is another very important metric.\N尖对尖是另一个非常重要的指标。
Dialogue: 0,0:39:45.27,0:39:49.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is single shot EUV lithography.\N这是单次曝光 EUV 光刻。
Dialogue: 0,0:39:49.35,0:39:53.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,Not only can we do metal layers, but also the contact layers.\N我们不仅能实现金属层，还能实现接触层。
Dialogue: 0,0:39:53.13,0:40:01.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,These are 30 nanometer contact layers from center to center, so the nominal CD is 15.\N这些是中心到中心 30 纳米的接触层，因此标称关键尺寸为 15。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:01.23,0:40:06.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is after lithography and this is after etch OK.\N这是光刻之后的结果，这是刻蚀之后的结果，明白了吗。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:06.03,0:40:12.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I mean, how best can you do this.\N所以，你认为怎样才能做到最好。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:12.19,0:40:13.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,What's the limit of this.\N这个技术的极限在哪里。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:13.85,0:40:20.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well I want to show you a slide that has a very small pitch.\N好，我想给大家看一张线距非常小的幻灯片。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:20.97,0:40:22.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is 8 nanometer half pitch.\N这是8纳米的半间距。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:22.69,0:40:24.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,So 16 nanometer pitch.\N因此，16纳米的间距。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:24.29,0:40:28.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,If you put it in the formula the K1 is 0.33.\N如果代入公式，K1值为0.33。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:28.17,0:40:31.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,Still we're not close to K1 equal to 0.25.\N但我们仍未接近K1等于0.25。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:31.29,0:40:33.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,But you can see we could do it.\N但你可以看到我们能做到。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:33.33,0:40:37.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,But of course, the image is of low contrast.\N当然，图像对比度很低。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:37.11,0:40:39.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,How do we.\N我们该如何解决。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:39.46,0:40:41.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,This cannot be in the manufacturing.\N这在制造中是不可能的。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:41.78,0:40:44.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,The contrast has to be a lot higher.\N对比度必须高得多。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:44.10,0:40:46.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,And how do we increase the contracts.\N我们如何提高对比度。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:46.00,0:40:48.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this will lead to the next part of the talk.\N这将引出接下来的演讲部分。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:48.40,0:40:50.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'd like to invite my colleague Ronald.\N我想邀请我的同事罗纳德。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:53.66,0:40:54.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,Thank you.\N谢谢。
Dialogue: 0,0:40:58.18,0:41:01.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,Tony and.\N安东尼和。
Dialogue: 0,0:41:01.26,0:41:05.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,Remember that Tony talked about customers asking tough questions.\N记住，安东尼提到过客户提出了非常棘手的问题。
Dialogue: 0,0:41:05.96,0:41:10.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,Do you get good resolution and can you make it cheaper.\N你能获得更好的分辨率，还能降低成本吗？
Dialogue: 0,0:41:10.36,0:41:14.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that's ultimately the business that we're in better resolution.\N这正是我们所处的业务核心——更高的分辨率。
Dialogue: 0,0:41:14.70,0:41:21.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,Tony talked about the formula that the minimum half pitch is K1 lambda over.\N安东尼提到了一个公式，最小半间距等于 K1 乘以波长除以。
Dialogue: 0,0:41:21.06,0:41:25.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,So in general try to go to a shorter wavelength.\N一般来说，尽量采用更短波长。
Dialogue: 0,0:41:25.06,0:41:32.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's always very complicated to go from 193 to 13.5 what was the timeline.\N从 193 纳米过渡到 13.5 纳米始终非常复杂，这个时间线是怎样的？
Dialogue: 0,0:41:32.98,0:41:35.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,How many years did you say 35 years.\N你说的是 35 年吗？
Dialogue: 0,0:41:35.88,0:41:40.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we're going to stick with 13.5 nanometers for quite a while.\N所以我们将在相当长的一段时间内继续使用 13.5 纳米。
Dialogue: 0,0:41:40.68,0:41:43.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,The next thing that you can do is go to a larger.\N你可以做的下一件事是采用更大的。
Dialogue: 0,0:41:43.20,0:41:45.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,But you already saw how expensive that is.\N但你已经看到了，这有多昂贵。
Dialogue: 0,0:41:45.26,0:41:48.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then you have the third factor, the K1.\N然后你还有第三个因素，即 K1。
Dialogue: 0,0:41:48.44,0:41:54.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I kind of feel a little bit pretentious being here,\N我觉得有点自命不凡，居然能站在这里，
Dialogue: 0,0:41:54.46,0:42:02.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,because then we start talking about computational lithography and who am I daring to show up here at Berkeley, where\N因为接下来我们就要讨论计算光刻，而我算哪根葱，竟敢出现在伯克利，这里
Dialogue: 0,0:42:02.72,0:42:08.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,one of your professors, Andy neureuter he's really the godfather of computational lithography.\N你们的一位教授安迪·诺伊特勒，他才是计算光刻的奠基人。
Dialogue: 0,0:42:08.82,0:42:12.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I'm basically preaching to the choir.\N所以我基本上是在对懂行的人讲话。
Dialogue: 0,0:42:12.36,0:42:16.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,But allow me anyway to share my view on some of this.\N但请允许我仍然分享一下我的看法。
Dialogue: 0,0:42:16.92,0:42:28.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,Obviously, we stand on the shoulders of many Giants that have helped with developing a lot of the theory all the way from Fresnel, Fourier, Maxwell, Helmholtz, Ernst\N显然，我们站在许多巨人的肩膀上，他们为发展大量理论做出了贡献，\N从菲涅尔、傅里叶、麦克斯韦、赫尔姆霍兹、恩斯特
Dialogue: 0,0:42:28.36,0:42:39.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,Abbe there, this guy here that was one of the first chief scientists of Zeiss, did a lot of work on microscopy and developed it from there.\N阿贝，这位蔡司的早期首席科学家之一，他在显微镜领域做了大量工作，并由此推动了该领域的发展。
Dialogue: 0,0:42:42.74,0:42:43.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,What did he say.\N他说了什么？
Dialogue: 0,0:42:43.56,0:42:46.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,He say in 1873 already.\N早在1873年他就说过。
Dialogue: 0,0:42:46.48,0:42:55.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,Hey, the minimum pitch is 1/2 times the wavelength divided by the sine of that opening angle that we now call the.\N嗨，最小间距等于波长的一半，除以那个我们如今称为数值孔径的开口角的正弦值。
Dialogue: 0,0:42:55.62,0:42:58.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,And he didn't have the refractive index yet.\N而那时他还没有折射率的概念。
Dialogue: 0,0:42:58.20,0:43:03.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now we say that n times the sine of that angle is the numerical aperture.\N现在我们说，n乘以该角度的正弦值就是数值孔径。
Dialogue: 0,0:43:03.14,0:43:06.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,But isn't it remarkable.\N但难道不令人惊叹吗？
Dialogue: 0,0:43:06.42,0:43:19.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,Since 1873 to the day of today, a very simple formula has governed our roadmap, and the rest was simply technology.\N从1873年到今天，一个非常简单的公式一直指导着我们的技术路线图，其余的都只是技术问题。
Dialogue: 0,0:43:19.14,0:43:22.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, technology is not always simple.\N但技术并不总是简单的。
Dialogue: 0,0:43:22.50,0:43:31.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,Lin from TSMC said, hey, lambda and Na, that's physics and that's geometry of your lenses.\N台积电的林先生说，嘿，波长λ和数值孔径Na，这都是光学和镜头的几何特性。
Dialogue: 0,0:43:31.30,0:43:38.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,But that K1, that's all kind of illumination tricks chemistry, resist contrast, aberrations, and mask tricks.\N但那个k1值，涉及各种照明技巧、化学工艺、光刻胶对比度、像差以及掩模技巧。
Dialogue: 0,0:43:38.89,0:43:42.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the minimum value that you can accomplish is 0.25.\N你能达到的最小值是0.25。
Dialogue: 0,0:43:42.93,0:43:50.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I'm going to talk about what can we all do to reduce that K1 if we want to do that with computation.\N所以我将谈谈我们能做些什么来通过计算手段降低这个k1值。
Dialogue: 0,0:43:50.81,0:43:54.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,It starts with I need to have a good model for what am I actually getting.\N这始于我需要一个良好的模型，来准确反映我实际获得的结果。
Dialogue: 0,0:43:54.83,0:44:06.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I need a forward model with which I can predict what is the quality of the imaging that I get and that good model that will involve a MAST model.\N我需要一个前向模型，能够预测我所获得的成像质量，而这个优质模型将涉及一个MAST模型。
Dialogue: 0,0:44:06.57,0:44:10.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,I need to see what is happening in my optics.\N我需要观察光学系统中发生了什么。
Dialogue: 0,0:44:10.05,0:44:12.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,What is the reaction of my resist.\N我需要了解光刻胶的反应情况。
Dialogue: 0,0:44:12.23,0:44:13.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's a lot of chemistry.\N这涉及大量的化学知识。
Dialogue: 0,0:44:13.65,0:44:16.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,And ultimately, I don't care what the pattern is, am I resist.\N最终，我不在乎这个模式是什么，我就是抗拒。
Dialogue: 0,0:44:16.95,0:44:19.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,I want it to have it in a device layer.\N我希望它能存在于器件层中。
Dialogue: 0,0:44:19.35,0:44:23.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I need an edge model that becomes my forward model.\N因此我需要一个边缘模型，作为我的前向模型。
Dialogue: 0,0:44:23.69,0:44:30.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,And a lot of effort has gone into how do I make that forward model as good as I can.\N为此投入了大量努力，以使这个前向模型尽可能完善。
Dialogue: 0,0:44:30.45,0:44:32.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,And we continue to improve with that.\N我们仍在持续改进。
Dialogue: 0,0:44:32.83,0:44:35.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,More and more physics, more and more chemistry.\N越来越多的物理，越来越多的化学。
Dialogue: 0,0:44:35.37,0:44:39.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,And at some point in time, we said, hey, AI is starting to take off.\N某个时刻，我们意识到，AI 开始兴起。
Dialogue: 0,0:44:39.37,0:44:41.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,And perhaps when we have a lot of good measurements,\N或许当我们拥有大量优质测量数据时，
Dialogue: 0,0:44:41.95,0:44:53.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,we can develop an AI model that can predict what I will get, even without fully understanding why that is what I will get and all of that is being employed.\N我们可以开发出一个AI模型，预测我将得到的结果，即使尚未完全理解其背后的原因，而这一切正在被应用。
Dialogue: 0,0:44:53.19,0:44:57.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,But once I have such a good model, I can go and play all kinds of tricks.\N但一旦我拥有了这样一个出色的模型，我就能施展各种手段。
Dialogue: 0,0:44:57.79,0:45:07.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,I can make a little manufacturing check where with a little layer and a little model that I have developed, I can now say, what am I going to get on the wafer.\N我可以进行一次小小的制造检查，仅凭一个薄层和我开发出的一个模型，现在就能预测我在晶圆上将得到什么。
Dialogue: 0,0:45:07.49,0:45:11.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,And is that what I wanted in the first place.\N那正是我最初想要的东西吗。
Dialogue: 0,0:45:11.23,0:45:17.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,Another option is that I say, hey, I have a little layer and I have measurements on what is the wafer.\N另一种选择是，我说，嘿，我有一个小层，我有晶圆上的测量数据。
Dialogue: 0,0:45:17.21,0:45:22.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,I can calibrate that model or I have a little model.\N我可以校准这个模型，或者我有一个小模型。
Dialogue: 0,0:45:22.47,0:45:24.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,I know what I want on the wafer.\N我知道我想要在晶圆上实现什么。
Dialogue: 0,0:45:24.83,0:45:30.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,What do I need to put on my reticle so that I get, in the end, what I wanted in the first place.\N我需要在掩模上放置什么，才能最终得到我最初想要的结果。
Dialogue: 0,0:45:30.79,0:45:33.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you contributed quite a bit of that.\N你在其中贡献了不少。
Dialogue: 0,0:45:33.68,0:45:36.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's where the field of inverse lithography and so on.\N这就是逆光刻等领域所在。
Dialogue: 0,0:45:36.72,0:45:38.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'm going to talk about a different one today.\N今天我要讲的是另一个不同的方向。
Dialogue: 0,0:45:38.84,0:45:40.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that's this one here.\N就是这个。
Dialogue: 0,0:45:40.26,0:45:42.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,I know what I want on the wafer.\N我知道我想要在晶圆上实现什么。
Dialogue: 0,0:45:42.12,0:45:43.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,How can I optimize.\N我该如何优化。
Dialogue: 0,0:45:43.64,0:45:46.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,What is the pattern that I put on the little layer.\N我在小层上放置的图案应该是什么。
Dialogue: 0,0:45:46.34,0:45:49.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,And how do I instruct my scanner.\N我该如何指导我的扫描仪。
Dialogue: 0,0:45:49.68,0:45:58.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,What is the illumination that I will use on the scanner to maximize the contrast to get the best possible results.\N我应该使用怎样的照明，才能最大化对比度，获得最佳结果。
Dialogue: 0,0:45:58.46,0:46:05.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now, when we talk about illumination, let me talk for a moment about an illumination system.\N当我们谈到照明时，让我先简单介绍一下照明系统。
Dialogue: 0,0:46:05.06,0:46:12.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,Trick is what I would like to have is very, very uniform illumination of my mask.\N我希望我的掩模获得非常非常均匀的照明。
Dialogue: 0,0:46:12.14,0:46:21.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,And in order to play with interference and to play with diffraction and be able to manipulate exactly what is the interference pattern\N为了操控干涉和衍射，并精确控制在晶圆上形成的干涉图样，
Dialogue: 0,0:46:21.94,0:46:29.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,that I get on the wafer, I need to make sure that this is spatially coherent light.\N我必须确保这是空间相干光。
Dialogue: 0,0:46:29.22,0:46:30.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,And how do we do that.\N我们是如何做到这一点的？
Dialogue: 0,0:46:30.76,0:46:33.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,We create this so-called 4f system.\N我们构建了所谓的4f系统。
Dialogue: 0,0:46:33.80,0:46:39.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,A guy Kohler invented this by having this four lens system.\N科勒先生通过这种四透镜系统发明了它。
Dialogue: 0,0:46:39.60,0:46:48.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,What I can create is, for every point in the light source, there is one particular set of planar waves that hits the mask.\N对于光源中的每一个点，都会有一组特定的平面波照射到掩模上。
Dialogue: 0,0:46:48.84,0:46:58.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,So if I imagine myself sitting on the mask and I look back at the illumination, imagine a circle\N如果我想象自己坐在掩模上，回头看向照明系统，想象一个圆形，
Dialogue: 0,0:46:58.16,0:47:01.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,and I could get light from that whole circle at me.\N我就能从整个圆周接收到光线。
Dialogue: 0,0:47:01.18,0:47:03.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,That would be a cone of light.\N那将是一束光锥。
Dialogue: 0,0:47:03.36,0:47:06.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,I could have light just coming from the center of that point.\N我可以让光仅从该点中心射出。
Dialogue: 0,0:47:06.86,0:47:09.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,That would be one planar wave.\N那将是一束平面波。
Dialogue: 0,0:47:09.32,0:47:15.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,I will also show you that I can have light coming from there and from there, and then I get very particular diffraction.\N我还将向你们展示，我可以让光从那里和那里射出，从而产生非常特定的衍射。
Dialogue: 0,0:47:15.94,0:47:18.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that's all the optimization that we're going to do.\N这就是我们所要进行的所有优化。
Dialogue: 0,0:47:21.88,0:47:29.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,So here you see that a little bit for every point in my source plane in the illumination I now get a number.\N所以在这里，你看到对于光源平面中的每一个点，照明中现在都会对应一个数值。
Dialogue: 0,0:47:29.39,0:47:35.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,I got plane waves onto the mask that leads to diffraction.\N我将平面波投射到掩模上，从而产生衍射。
Dialogue: 0,0:47:35.43,0:47:38.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the diffraction orders are captured by the objective lens.\N衍射级次由物镜镜头捕捉。
Dialogue: 0,0:47:38.65,0:47:42.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the bigger this lens, the bigger these opening angles,\N镜头越大，这些开口角度就越大，
Dialogue: 0,0:47:42.01,0:47:49.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,the more of these diffraction patterns I capture, and the better will be the reconstruction of the image here on the image plane.\N我就能捕捉到越多的衍射图案，从而在像平面上的图像重建效果就越好。
Dialogue: 0,0:47:49.61,0:47:52.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's basically Fourier optics.\N这基本上就是傅里叶光学。
Dialogue: 0,0:47:52.59,0:48:03.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let's look a little bit more at diffraction for those of you that are not totally familiar with that, Mr. Huygens already said, hey, when you have light hitting an opening,\N所以让我们再深入一点看看衍射，对于那些不太熟悉这个概念的观众，\N惠更斯先生早就说过，当光照射到一个开口上时，
Dialogue: 0,0:48:03.03,0:48:08.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,each point here starts to act as a source of spherical waves,\N这里的每个点开始表现为球面波的源，
Dialogue: 0,0:48:08.51,0:48:19.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,and it's really the path optical path length differences that then create this pattern here, where in some directions I end up getting light, and in other directions I do not get light.\N真正造成这种图案的是光程差，导致某些方向上有光，而其他方向上没有光。
Dialogue: 0,0:48:19.61,0:48:22.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,And those are called the diffraction orders.\N这些被称为衍射级次。
Dialogue: 0,0:48:22.51,0:48:31.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,In essence, if I look at my aperture, the mask, and I think of that as a repeating pattern.\N本质上，如果我观察我的数值孔径、掩模，并将其视为一个重复的图案。
Dialogue: 0,0:48:31.05,0:48:38.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,I can take the Fourier transform of that pattern, and every diffraction order encodes one of the Fourier components.\N我可以对这个图案进行傅里叶变换，每个衍射级次都编码了傅里叶分量之一。
Dialogue: 0,0:48:38.49,0:48:41.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you can think of the lens as a filter.\N你可以把镜头看作是一种滤镜。
Dialogue: 0,0:48:41.25,0:48:45.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,How many of the Fourier components can I capture that lead.\N我能捕捉到多少个傅里叶分量，从而实现领先？
Dialogue: 0,0:48:45.31,0:48:48.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,Help me reconstruct the pattern of the mask.\N帮我重建掩模的图案。
Dialogue: 0,0:48:48.27,0:48:51.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now on the wafer.\N现在在晶圆上。
Dialogue: 0,0:48:51.73,0:48:57.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,So in certain directions, look at this wave from this opening.\N所以从某些方向看，注意这个从开口处发出的波。
Dialogue: 0,0:48:57.93,0:49:03.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,This one here had just this one was one wavelength to here this one was two.\N这个只有一个波长，这个有两个。
Dialogue: 0,0:49:03.43,0:49:04.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,This one was three.\N这个是三。
Dialogue: 0,0:49:04.57,0:49:13.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,So in this particular direction, all of these waves are in phase and they contribute to positive interference.\N因此，在这个特定方向上，所有这些波都同相，会产生建设性干涉。
Dialogue: 0,0:49:13.03,0:49:16.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that is called the Bragg condition.\N这被称为布拉格条件。
Dialogue: 0,0:49:16.97,0:49:25.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,Each of these diffraction patterns I can now project here in the pupil plane for the lens as a particular spot.\N我现在可以将这些衍射图样中的每一个，投影到透镜的孔径平面上，形成一个特定的光斑。
Dialogue: 0,0:49:25.37,0:49:30.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,And when these diffraction patterns start to fall outside the aperture of the lens,\N当这些衍射图样开始超出透镜的孔径范围，
Dialogue: 0,0:49:30.57,0:49:39.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,the numerical aperture of the lens, then no longer does it contribute to the image formation at the wafer level.\N也就是超出透镜的数值孔径时，它们就不再对晶圆层面的成像产生贡献。
Dialogue: 0,0:49:39.45,0:49:41.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,It basically looks like something like this.\N它看起来大致像这样。
Dialogue: 0,0:49:41.95,0:49:43.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,My zeroth order.\N我的零级衍射。
Dialogue: 0,0:49:43.09,0:49:46.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,The light that straight goes through these waves.\N指的是直接穿过这些光栅的光。
Dialogue: 0,0:49:46.49,0:49:49.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,They lead to no contrast here.\N它们在这里不会产生对比度。
Dialogue: 0,0:49:49.19,0:49:59.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,The first order is a wave with the same frequency as the pattern, the second order is triple the frequency, et cetera.\N一级衍射是与图案频率相同的波，二级衍射是三倍频率，依此类推。
Dialogue: 0,0:49:59.95,0:50:06.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,So that's how in the end, I reconstruct the image at the wafer plane.\N这就是我最终在晶圆平面上重建图像的方式。
Dialogue: 0,0:50:06.15,0:50:07.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'm going a little bit fast.\N我讲得有点快。
Dialogue: 0,0:50:07.57,0:50:19.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,So now Abe was the one that said so now my challenge is I know what I want as a wafer,\N现在，安倍说的是，我的挑战在于，我清楚自己想要的晶圆上的光强分布，
Dialogue: 0,0:50:19.63,0:50:23.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,as a light intensity on the wafer plane.\N也就是晶圆平面上的光强。
Dialogue: 0,0:50:23.07,0:50:31.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,My challenge is to optimize the source here so that for every source point, the waves hitting the mask\N我的挑战是优化这里的光源，使得每个光源点发出的波在经过掩模、通过透镜以及所有路径长度差异后，
Dialogue: 0,0:50:31.18,0:50:42.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,with how they go through the lens and all the path length differences, how that all adds up to this spatial frequency on the mask.\N最终叠加起来，正好形成掩模上的这个空间频率。
Dialogue: 0,0:50:42.34,0:50:44.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,Let me show that to you.\N让我给你们演示一下。
Dialogue: 0,0:50:44.00,0:50:45.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,Much simpler.\N简单多了。
Dialogue: 0,0:50:45.34,0:50:56.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,Here we have that one central point that I was talking about this large illumination disk hitting a mask, creating diffraction patterns.\N这里我们看到那个我之前提到的中心点，一个大的照明光斑照射到掩模上，产生衍射图案。
Dialogue: 0,0:50:56.44,0:51:00.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,Each of these diffraction patterns are within the pupil plane of the lens,\N这些衍射图案都位于透镜的孔径平面内，
Dialogue: 0,0:51:00.54,0:51:06.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,and therefore they contribute to this interference pattern on the wafer.\N因此它们共同贡献了晶圆上的这个干涉图案。
Dialogue: 0,0:51:06.82,0:51:10.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,What happens now when I start to reduce the pitch.\N现在我开始减小节距时会发生什么。
Dialogue: 0,0:51:12.66,0:51:17.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,You see that some of the diffraction now moves further out.\N你看到一些衍射图案现在向外移动得更远了。
Dialogue: 0,0:51:17.02,0:51:20.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,The contrast in my image starts to reduce.\N我图像的对比度开始下降。
Dialogue: 0,0:51:20.06,0:51:25.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I'm now at a CD basically of 0.5 lambda over an A.\N现在我处于一个相当于0.5倍波长除以A的CD值。
Dialogue: 0,0:51:25.48,0:51:30.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,If I now go any further with shrinking,\N如果我现在继续缩小尺寸，
Dialogue: 0,0:51:30.32,0:51:37.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,suddenly those outer points no longer are captured by the lens, and I have 0 contrast left.\N那些外侧的点突然就不再被镜头捕捉到，对比度完全消失。
Dialogue: 0,0:51:37.92,0:51:39.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,There's no imaging.\N图像无法形成。
Dialogue: 0,0:51:39.80,0:51:40.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,But wait a minute.\N但等一下。
Dialogue: 0,0:51:40.58,0:51:42.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,I can do some tricks.\N我可以玩些技巧。
Dialogue: 0,0:51:42.96,0:51:48.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,What if instead of using that point source, if I use a whole disk.\N如果我不使用点光源，而是用一个完整的圆盘呢？
Dialogue: 0,0:51:50.92,0:51:58.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now you see that right here at the edge, there is, again, a little bit of overlap between the zeroth order and the first order.\N现在你看到，在边缘位置，零级和一级光之间再次出现一点重叠。
Dialogue: 0,0:51:58.84,0:52:07.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I start to see a little bit of the reconstruction of my image at the wafer plane.\N因此，我开始在晶圆平面上看到一点图像重建。
Dialogue: 0,0:52:07.68,0:52:18.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,But the contrast is very low because there is a lot of light from the center that doesn't really contribute anything to the interference.\N但对比度很低，因为中心的大量光线并未真正参与干涉。
Dialogue: 0,0:52:18.68,0:52:21.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,So what if I could move that light.\N那如果我能移动这些光线呢？
Dialogue: 0,0:52:21.46,0:52:24.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,That would improve my contrast.\N这样就能提升对比度。
Dialogue: 0,0:52:24.27,0:52:28.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I go and use what we call annular illumination.\N于是我使用我们称之为环形照明的技术。
Dialogue: 0,0:52:28.31,0:52:31.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,Instead of the light coming out of that whole disk, I now see it.\N不再是从整个圆盘发出的光，我现在能看到它了。
Dialogue: 0,0:52:31.49,0:52:40.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,If I look from the mask back at the illumination, I would see it come from a ring and I get much better contrast.\N如果我从掩模看向照明，会发现光来自一个环形，对比度显著提高。
Dialogue: 0,0:52:40.71,0:52:44.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, if I can do that, why not do even more.\N既然能做到这一点，为什么不做得更进一步？
Dialogue: 0,0:52:44.11,0:52:48.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because that light here at the edges is also not contributing.\N因为边缘的光也并未发挥作用。
Dialogue: 0,0:52:48.27,0:52:51.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is where the positive interference come from.\N这正是正向干涉产生的地方。
Dialogue: 0,0:52:51.17,0:52:53.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let me remove that also.\N那么让我也把这部分去掉。
Dialogue: 0,0:52:53.95,0:52:56.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I get even better contrast.\N这样对比度进一步提升。
Dialogue: 0,0:52:56.43,0:53:00.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now I have something called dipole illumination.\N现在我有了所谓的双极照明。
Dialogue: 0,0:53:00.39,0:53:06.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that is what a lot of the fancy stuff that is happening nowadays.\N这正是当今许多先进光刻技术的核心。
Dialogue: 0,0:53:06.35,0:53:12.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,We can basically create with our illumination arbitrary shapes for the illumination.\N我们基本上可以通过照明系统，为光刻过程创建任意形状的照明。
Dialogue: 0,0:53:12.97,0:53:19.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,And what is the best illumination that will for the set of patterns lead to the best reconstruction.\N而最佳的照明方式，就是能让这一组图案实现最精确重构的照明。
Dialogue: 0,0:53:23.09,0:53:25.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,How does that then work out.\N那样的话是怎么运作的呢。
Dialogue: 0,0:53:25.13,0:53:33.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,If I have a full chip design, I will pick a bunch of critical patterns, the ones that I care about most.\N如果我有一个完整的芯片设计，我会挑选出一组关键图案，也就是我最关心的那些。
Dialogue: 0,0:53:33.53,0:53:36.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,For those, I will start doing this optimization.\N对这些图案，我会开始进行这种优化。
Dialogue: 0,0:53:36.51,0:53:39.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,I have a forward model, I keep calculating,\N我有一个前向模型，会持续进行计算。
Dialogue: 0,0:53:39.85,0:53:47.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,I can use inverse lithography and try to do a more direct calculation, but in the end, I will end up with an illumination.\N我可以使用逆光刻技术，尝试进行更直接的计算，但最终还是会得到一个照明方案。
Dialogue: 0,0:53:47.89,0:53:50.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,You can see that there at the top.\N你可以在上面看到这个。
Dialogue: 0,0:53:50.01,0:53:53.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,And with that illumination I can.\N拥有这个照明方案后，我就可以。
Dialogue: 0,0:53:53.69,0:53:54.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah there you go.\N对，就是这样。
Dialogue: 0,0:53:54.63,0:53:58.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,We start to improve, iterate on the illumination.\N我们开始改进并迭代照明方案。
Dialogue: 0,0:53:58.35,0:54:03.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you can now start seeing that as I project I get a cleaner and cleaner image.\N现在你可以看到，随着我进行投影，图像变得越来越清晰。
Dialogue: 0,0:54:03.65,0:54:12.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,Once I have the very best illumination, I can say, OK, now let me use with this illumination OPC to improve the whole mask.\N一旦我得到了最佳的照明方案，我就可以说，好了，现在让我用这个照明方案配合 OPC 来优化整个掩模。
Dialogue: 0,0:54:12.25,0:54:16.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,And if I did the original selection of the patterns that I optimized for.\N如果我当初选择了那些我优化过的图案。
Dialogue: 0,0:54:16.55,0:54:23.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, and those were really the most critical ones then this illumination will actually work for the whole chip.\N嗯，那些是最关键的，这样照明就能适用于整个芯片。
Dialogue: 0,0:54:26.63,0:54:30.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,So how does that optimization algorithm work.\N那么这个优化算法是如何工作的？
Dialogue: 0,0:54:30.55,0:54:39.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,Basically we tend to look at hey, I want to this is what the designer drew this square thing.\N基本上我们通常会考虑，我想实现的是设计者画出的这个方形结构。
Dialogue: 0,0:54:39.51,0:54:42.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now we know that we can never print something square.\N现在我们知道，永远无法打印出一个真正的方形。
Dialogue: 0,0:54:42.45,0:54:44.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,There's always finite resolution.\N总存在有限的分辨率。
Dialogue: 0,0:54:44.95,0:54:49.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,So if I would try to optimize directly to that square shape,\N所以如果我直接优化到这个方形形状，
Dialogue: 0,0:54:49.91,0:54:56.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,my total cost function, which is all the errors added up, would be completely dominated by these corners.\N我的总成本函数，也就是所有误差相加的结果，将完全被这些角落所主导。
Dialogue: 0,0:54:56.21,0:54:57.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that's artificial.\N这是人为造成的。
Dialogue: 0,0:54:57.57,0:55:03.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we say, theoretically the best that I can do is some target that I can draw here.\N所以我们说，理论上我能做到的最好情况是这里画出的一个目标形状。
Dialogue: 0,0:55:03.35,0:55:13.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,Let me now try to get the mask and the illumination so that I get as close as possible to what I think is possible, and I want to do that for a range of conditions,\N现在我要尝试找到掩模和照明，使结果尽可能接近我认为可能实现的形状，并且要在一系列条件下实现这一点，
Dialogue: 0,0:55:13.57,0:55:18.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,not just for nominal, but also for a little bit more or less dose.\N而不仅仅是标准条件，还包括剂量稍多或稍少的情况。
Dialogue: 0,0:55:18.90,0:55:21.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,Dose is never perfectly controlled.\N剂量永远无法完全精确控制。
Dialogue: 0,0:55:21.18,0:55:23.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,A little bit more.\N稍微多一点。
Dialogue: 0,0:55:23.46,0:55:27.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,Some focus variation and the mask making is also not perfect.\N有一些焦点变化，掩模制作也不完美。
Dialogue: 0,0:55:27.72,0:55:37.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I take all these variances into account and make sure that I arrive at something that is robust under these variations.\N因此我考虑了所有这些变化因素，确保最终结果在这些变化下依然稳健。
Dialogue: 0,0:55:37.18,0:55:39.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,Here are some of the masks that I will skip.\N这里有一些我会跳过的掩模。
Dialogue: 0,0:55:39.94,0:55:45.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,Here are some of the math that I will also skip that determines how all of that works.\N这里有一些我会跳过的数学计算，它们决定了整个过程的工作原理。
Dialogue: 0,0:55:45.24,0:55:56.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,But what I wanted to point out here is there are actually two terms here that is this here that captures some of the effects from non chemically amplified resist.\N但我想指出的是，这里实际上有两个项，其中这一项捕捉了非化学放大光刻胶的一些效应。
Dialogue: 0,0:55:56.46,0:56:01.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I want right now to focus your attention here for a moment at this term.\N现在我想请大家暂时关注这一项。
Dialogue: 0,0:56:01.58,0:56:03.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is a mask 3D effect.\N这是掩模的三维效应。
Dialogue: 0,0:56:03.82,0:56:07.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,So what is a mask 3D effect.\N那么什么是掩模的三维效应？
Dialogue: 0,0:56:07.22,0:56:11.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'll go fast I'm nearly done.\N我会讲得快一些，我快讲完了。
Dialogue: 0,0:56:11.06,0:56:13.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,Here is one of those poles of the illumination.\N这是照明中的一个极点。
Dialogue: 0,0:56:13.80,0:56:16.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,The zeroth order is light that goes through.\N零阶是直接通过的光。
Dialogue: 0,0:56:16.42,0:56:18.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,It goes to my wafer.\N它会落在我的晶圆上。
Dialogue: 0,0:56:18.52,0:56:22.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,Here is the first diffraction order and it goes here.\N这是第一个衍射级次，它会到这里。
Dialogue: 0,0:56:22.48,0:56:29.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, because of electromagnetic effects here, there is an extra phase difference between these two.\N由于这里的电磁效应，这两者之间会产生额外的相位差。
Dialogue: 0,0:56:29.72,0:56:31.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's called a mask 3D effect.\N这被称为掩模三维效应。
Dialogue: 0,0:56:31.86,0:56:40.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that means that instead of a really good reconstruction of all my Fourier components, imagine that I have a Fourier transform.\N这意味着，而不是完美地重建我所有的傅里叶分量，想象一下我有一个傅里叶变换。
Dialogue: 0,0:56:40.74,0:56:50.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,But I'm a little bit off on the amplitude of some of these components, or I'm a little bit off on the phase of these components that would start to affect your reconstruction.\N但这些分量中有些的振幅略有偏差，或者这些分量的相位略有偏差，这将开始影响你的重建效果。
Dialogue: 0,0:56:50.84,0:56:55.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,So our goal is to reconstruct how can I do that correctly.\N因此，我们的目标是正确地重建出这种现象。
Dialogue: 0,0:56:55.14,0:56:58.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because my one pole remember the dipole illumination.\N因为我记得，我的照明是偶极子照明。
Dialogue: 0,0:56:58.52,0:56:59.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,This was one pole.\N这是一个偶极子。
Dialogue: 0,0:56:59.92,0:57:04.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,And as a result of that phase difference you actually get a shift in your mask.\N由于这种相位差，实际上你的掩模会出现偏移。
Dialogue: 0,0:57:04.98,0:57:10.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,You see this phi minus phi be that phase difference leads to a shift in your mask.\N你看到这个 φ 减 φ′，这个相位差导致了掩模的偏移。
Dialogue: 0,0:57:10.10,0:57:16.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,Instead of the intensity being focused around the ideal position, it has shifted a little bit.\N本来强度应该集中在理想位置，但它已经发生了一点偏移。
Dialogue: 0,0:57:16.79,0:57:20.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now the other pole will have this shift in the other direction.\N现在另一个光斑将朝相反方向发生偏移。
Dialogue: 0,0:57:20.83,0:57:27.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,So my total image is now the sum of two distributions.\N因此，我的整体图像现在是两个分布的总和。
Dialogue: 0,0:57:27.63,0:57:32.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,If they were perfectly aligned I would have a nice sharp image, but they are not.\N如果它们完全对齐，我将得到一个清晰锐利的图像，但它们并非如此。
Dialogue: 0,0:57:32.25,0:57:36.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the image fades I get a reduction in contrast.\N所以图像变得模糊，对比度降低。
Dialogue: 0,0:57:36.91,0:57:38.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,What could I do.\N我能做些什么呢？
Dialogue: 0,0:57:38.15,0:57:40.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,Could I do something smart.\N我能不能做点聪明的事？
Dialogue: 0,0:57:40.63,0:57:49.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,What if I have the illumination and instead of the dipole here, I move this pole down a little bit.\N如果我调整照明方式，不再把这种双极结构放在这里，而是把这个光斑稍微往下移一点。
Dialogue: 0,0:57:49.73,0:57:56.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,I move this pole up a little bit, and in my lens I can now compensate for the mask's 3D effects.\N我把这个光斑稍微往上移一点，这样在透镜中我就能补偿掩模的三维效应。
Dialogue: 0,0:57:56.87,0:58:06.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because this order here, these diffraction orders here and here are the zeroth order.\N因为这里的这些衍射级次，也就是这里的零级衍射。
Dialogue: 0,0:58:06.29,0:58:13.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,And now I can have the one pole shift to the left, the other one a little bit to the right.\N现在我可以让一个光斑向左移动，另一个稍微向右移动。
Dialogue: 0,0:58:13.33,0:58:16.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,And now they overlap again perfectly.\N这样它们又能完美重叠了。
Dialogue: 0,0:58:16.49,0:58:18.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now that's simple.\N现在这就简单了。
Dialogue: 0,0:58:18.17,0:58:22.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,If I only have to do with a 2D pattern, what we can now do.\N如果我只需要处理一个二维图案，我们现在能做到的事情。
Dialogue: 0,0:58:22.75,0:58:26.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that's why we have computers, I can actually generalize this.\N这就是为什么我们需要计算机，我实际上可以将这一方法泛化。
Dialogue: 0,0:58:26.95,0:58:30.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I can take an arbitrary mask pattern for logic.\N我可以为逻辑电路任意设定一个掩模图案。
Dialogue: 0,0:58:30.33,0:58:42.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I can say, hey, what would now be the very best aberration pattern in my lens that would make that all these images from these poles overlap as well as possible.\N我可以问，现在我的镜头中什么样的像差模式能最好地使这些来自不同光路的图像尽可能重合。
Dialogue: 0,0:58:42.53,0:58:44.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that's what you see here.\N这就是你们在这里看到的。
Dialogue: 0,0:58:44.41,0:58:51.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,Here is what I just explained for a simple if all I look at is lens is lines and spaces.\N这是我刚才用一个简单情况说明的，即我只关注镜头中的线条和间距。
Dialogue: 0,0:58:51.85,0:58:56.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is for the actual mask pattern here.\N这是针对这里实际掩模图案的情况。
Dialogue: 0,0:58:56.33,0:59:03.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,And as a result, I get much better exposure, latitude and much better adaptive focus in my final imaging.\N因此，我最终获得的曝光宽容度更高，焦点适应性也显著提升。
Dialogue: 0,0:59:03.69,0:59:12.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I hope that with that, I've given you a little bit of a flavor on how we can use computation to optimize the settings on the scanner,\N所以我希望通过这些内容，能让你们对如何利用计算优化光刻机的设置有了一点直观感受。
Dialogue: 0,0:59:12.29,0:59:16.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,both from an illumination perspective as well as aberrations in the lens.\N无论是从照明角度，还是镜头像差方面。
Dialogue: 0,0:59:16.75,0:59:24.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,We try to minimize the aberrations, and sometimes we introduce them on purpose to correct for mass 3D effects.\N我们通常尽量减少像差，但有时也会故意引入像差，以补偿掩模的三维效应。
Dialogue: 0,0:59:24.95,0:59:28.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now, with all of that said, let me finish up the last part.\N话虽如此，让我来结束最后一部分。
Dialogue: 0,0:59:28.67,0:59:33.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,Just a slide on the roadmap and the outlook.\N仅简单介绍一下路线图和展望。
Dialogue: 0,0:59:33.03,0:59:38.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,Remember the best possible resolution at the lowest possible price.\N记住，要在尽可能低的价格下实现最佳分辨率。
Dialogue: 0,0:59:38.67,0:59:43.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let's look at we've looked at the best possible resolution.\N那么我们来看一下，我们已经实现了最佳可能的分辨率。
Dialogue: 0,0:59:43.81,0:59:49.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Reduced the lambda, increase DNA decrease K1.\N降低λ值，提升DNA，降低K1。
Dialogue: 0,0:59:49.15,0:59:57.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,Let's now look for a moment that cost of the technology the productivity the EUV light source.\N现在我们来看一下这项技术的成本、生产效率以及EUV光源。
Dialogue: 0,0:59:57.99,1:00:07.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,We now we've recently announced that our latest results in San Diego, we can get one kilowatt of power out of our dose.\N我们最近在圣地亚哥宣布了最新成果，我们已能从剂量中获得一千瓦的功率。
Dialogue: 0,1:00:07.29,1:00:13.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,And remember what Tony said in the beginning we struggled to get one Watt.\N记住托尼一开始说的，我们曾为获得一瓦功率而挣扎。
Dialogue: 0,1:00:13.58,1:00:17.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,Then at some point we were really excited that we got 10 watts.\N后来我们曾非常兴奋，终于达到了十瓦。
Dialogue: 0,1:00:17.78,1:00:23.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,TSMC told us that when we hit 80w, we can go into volume production.\N台积电告诉我们，当达到80瓦时，我们就可以进入量产。
Dialogue: 0,1:00:23.60,1:00:26.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,That was in 2019.\N那是在2019年。
Dialogue: 0,1:00:26.30,1:00:32.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,That was 2 watts or that was 281 was 2014 was 214.\N那是2瓦，或者说是2014年的281瓦，2014年是214瓦。
Dialogue: 0,1:00:32.08,1:00:33.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK yeah.\N好的，没错。
Dialogue: 0,1:00:33.04,1:00:37.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,Sorry so that's when they said, OK, now we are serious about it.\N对不起，所以他们说，好吧，现在我们认真对待这件事了。
Dialogue: 0,1:00:37.92,1:00:39.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,We think that you can get there.\N我们认为你们能做到这一点。
Dialogue: 0,1:00:39.70,1:00:43.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,And by 2019 we were at 250.\N到2019年，我们已经达到了250。
Dialogue: 0,1:00:43.58,1:00:51.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,We now have in our hands demonstrations that we can actually hit 1 kilowatt.\N现在我们手头已经有演示，证明我们实际上可以达到1千瓦。
Dialogue: 0,1:00:51.22,1:00:58.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,Another way of reducing the cost of the system is by having more commonality between EUV.\N降低系统成本的另一种方式是提高EUV之间的通用性。
Dialogue: 0,1:00:58.70,1:01:05.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,Sorry, between the .33 and a system and the .55 and a system.\N对不起，是指.33与系统之间，以及.55与系统之间的通用性。
Dialogue: 0,1:01:05.30,1:01:16.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we have a roadmap on how we can create a more common platform that we will do that with also an option to in the future,\N因此我们有一条路线图，说明如何创建一个更通用的平台，我们也将为此提供未来选项，
Dialogue: 0,1:01:16.22,1:01:25.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,start looking at 0.75, for which we already have some prototype designs and where we can get at the end.\N开始考虑0.75，我们已经有一些原型设计，最终可以实现这一点。
Dialogue: 0,1:01:25.56,1:01:37.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,Or here is an example of with 13.5 nanometer EUV light from Paul scherrer Institute interferometric EUV lithography, where you do not have mass 3D effects.\N这里是一个例子，来自保罗·谢勒研究所的13.\N5纳米EUV光的干涉式EUV光刻，其中没有大规模3D效应。
Dialogue: 0,1:01:37.22,1:01:41.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,If we could really control everything can see that.\N如果我们真的能控制一切，就能看到这一点。
Dialogue: 0,1:01:41.64,1:01:51.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, there are already some evidence that half pitches of about five or six nanometer as a absolute theoretical maximum would be possible.\N是的，已经有了一些证据表明，五或六纳米左右的半间距，作为绝对理论极限是可能的。
Dialogue: 0,1:01:51.20,1:01:58.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we still have quite a ways to go that we can scale more than where we are today.\N所以我们还有很长的路要走，才能实现比今天更大的扩展。
Dialogue: 0,1:01:58.28,1:02:03.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,And with that, I come to the end, and I hope that I fulfilled my promise that I wouldn't be too long.\N话说到这儿，我就讲完了，希望我没有违背承诺，讲得太长。
Dialogue: 0,1:02:07.08,1:02:09.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,Tony, why don't you join me for questions.\N托尼，你为什么不和我一起回答问题呢？
Dialogue: 0,1:02:12.31,1:02:14.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,Hello what happened here.\N你好，这里发生了什么？
Dialogue: 0,1:02:14.83,1:02:15.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,No it's fine.\N没关系的。
Dialogue: 0,1:02:15.83,1:02:19.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,He was supposed to activate from that.\N他本该从那个地方启动的。
Dialogue: 0,1:02:19.15,1:02:20.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,All right.\N好的。
Dialogue: 0,1:02:20.03,1:02:21.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,Oh, I think it's on now.\N噢，我觉得现在打开了。
Dialogue: 0,1:02:21.23,1:02:28.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, so we'll take questions now for both speakers, and we're open for questions.\N好，现在我们将为两位演讲者回答问题，欢迎大家提问。
Dialogue: 0,1:02:32.15,1:02:36.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,Do I understand correctly that if the end user cares about,\N我理解得对吗，如果终端用户只关心某一轴向的分辨率，
Dialogue: 0,1:02:36.41,1:02:42.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,let's say, resolution just along one axis, that's an easier problem from your perspective.\N从你们的角度来看，这个问题会更容易解决。
Dialogue: 0,1:02:42.04,1:02:48.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,Is that correct to say, is that something that the end users are trying to or you all collectively are trying to leverage.\N说得对吗？这是终端用户在尝试的，还是你们大家共同在尝试利用的？
Dialogue: 0,1:02:48.45,1:02:51.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, so let's define first what the end user is, right.\N好，那我们先来定义一下什么是终端用户，对吧。
Dialogue: 0,1:02:51.79,1:03:01.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,If the end user is Apple then they tell TSMC it's your problem.\N如果最终用户是苹果公司，他们就会告诉台积电，这是你们的问题。
Dialogue: 0,1:03:01.75,1:03:11.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,TSMC will tell us, hey, do you we want to use dipole illumination combination, because that gives the very best contrast for one dimensional patterns.\N台积电会告诉我们，嘿，我们是否要使用偶极照明组合，因为这对一维图案能提供最佳对比度。
Dialogue: 0,1:03:11.53,1:03:18.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,Can you make a light source where all of the light only comes from these two points in that whole disk.\N你能制造一种光源，让整个圆盘中只有这两个点发出光线吗？
Dialogue: 0,1:03:18.97,1:03:29.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, we can do that pretty well, but if you make these spots too small, we can only do that by throwing some of the light away.\N我们能做到相当不错，但如果把这两个光点做得太小，就只能通过舍弃部分光线来实现。
Dialogue: 0,1:03:29.53,1:03:34.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,And if we throw some of the light away, then it goes at the expense of productivity.\N而如果我们舍弃部分光线，就会牺牲生产效率。
Dialogue: 0,1:03:34.13,1:03:42.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so our newer illumination designs can use a much smaller, what we call a pupil fill ratio.\N因此，我们新的照明设计可以使用更小的、我们称之为瞳孔填充率的数值。
Dialogue: 0,1:03:42.51,1:03:48.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,What's the smallest fraction of that whole illumination disk that you can define.\N你能定义的整个照明圆盘中最小的光强比例是多少？
Dialogue: 0,1:03:48.31,1:03:53.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's where I want the light to come from and still use 100% of the light.\N我希望光线从这里发出，同时还能100%利用所有光线。
Dialogue: 0,1:03:53.65,1:04:00.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,So it's really a shared responsibility between us and the customer.\N所以这实际上是英伟达和客户之间的共同责任。
Dialogue: 0,1:04:00.05,1:04:07.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,But for every layer metal 0, metal one, metal 2 contact holes, vias.\N但每一层，包括金属层0、金属层1、金属层2、接触孔和通孔。
Dialogue: 0,1:04:07.16,1:04:10.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,Every layer has its own illumination.\N每一层都有其独立的照明方案。
Dialogue: 0,1:04:10.20,1:04:13.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,And let me take your example a little bit to an extreme.\N让我稍微把你的例子推向一个极端。
Dialogue: 0,1:04:13.76,1:04:20.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,If I want the absolute smallest pitch in one direction,\N如果我想在某一方向上实现最小的间距，
Dialogue: 0,1:04:20.44,1:04:27.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,I would use a dipole with only two tiny little pieces of light here.\N我会使用一个仅包含两个微小光点的偶极子。
Dialogue: 0,1:04:27.44,1:04:34.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,I would now have great contrast in this direction and 0 contrast in the other direction.\N这样我就能在这个方向上获得很高的对比度，而在另一个方向上对比度为零。
Dialogue: 0,1:04:34.78,1:04:39.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,So the only thing that I can now do is print lines that are infinitely long.\N因此我现在唯一能做的就是打印无限长的线条。
Dialogue: 0,1:04:39.56,1:04:42.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,Not a single designer is interested in that.\N没有任何一位设计师会对这种结果感兴趣。
Dialogue: 0,1:04:42.64,1:04:49.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,So now what you could do is well, but if I have those very long lines, I will use a second exposure to cut them.\N所以现在你可以这么做：如果我有这些非常长的线条，我可以用第二次曝光来切割它们。
Dialogue: 0,1:04:49.20,1:04:50.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,But that's expensive.\N但这成本很高。
Dialogue: 0,1:04:50.62,1:04:54.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,Perhaps bring in these poles a little bit, regain a little bit of that contrast.\N或许可以稍微调整一下这些极点的位置，重新获得一些对比度。
Dialogue: 0,1:04:54.94,1:04:59.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,And now I can also print gaps in these lines.\N这样我就能在这些线条中打印出间隔。
Dialogue: 0,1:04:59.96,1:05:02.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,But how small I can get that gap.\N但这个间隔最小能到多小？
Dialogue: 0,1:05:02.46,1:05:10.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's the so-called tip to tip difference that depends on how you optimize the illumination and how you optimize the OPC.\N这就是所谓的尖端到尖端间距，它取决于照明优化和 OPC 优化的方式。
Dialogue: 0,1:05:10.90,1:05:15.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,So it's a very interactive discussion between us and our customers.\N所以这是我们与客户之间非常互动的讨论。
Dialogue: 0,1:05:15.46,1:05:17.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,Hope that answers your question.\N希望这能回答你的问题。
Dialogue: 0,1:05:17.08,1:05:18.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, here's another question.\N好的，这是另一个问题。
Dialogue: 0,1:05:18.44,1:05:19.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,Just a quick question.\N只提个快速问题。
Dialogue: 0,1:05:19.96,1:05:33.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,So if the minimum pitch you're expecting is like 90 nanometers, that does bring up the question, what exactly does the metric in like a node name refer to.\N所以如果你期望的最小线距大约是90纳米，这就引出了一个问题，即节点名称中的指标具体指的是什么。
Dialogue: 0,1:05:33.92,1:05:38.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,Because it's clearly not the pitch of the features themselves.\N因为显然它并不是特征本身的线距。
Dialogue: 0,1:05:38.34,1:05:39.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,Great question.\N很好的问题。
Dialogue: 0,1:05:39.48,1:05:46.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah so the node names right 5 nanometer node, 4 nanometer node three nanometer node.\N是的，节点名称如5纳米节点、4纳米节点、3纳米节点。
Dialogue: 0,1:05:46.22,1:05:54.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,Back in the days that we had the 45 nanometer node that actually really represented the size of the lines.\N以前我们有45纳米节点时，那确实代表了线条的实际尺寸。
Dialogue: 0,1:05:54.18,1:05:58.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,And at some point that became very increasingly difficult.\N但后来这变得越来越困难。
Dialogue: 0,1:05:58.38,1:06:10.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,But people also became very creative in terms of with design tricks and layout tricks, I can get more density out of the size that I have.\N不过人们在设计技巧和版图技巧方面也变得非常有创意，我可以用现有的尺寸实现更高的密度。
Dialogue: 0,1:06:10.48,1:06:20.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,So think of the 5 nanometer and the 4 nanometer and 3 nanometer now more as an indication of what is the effective transistor density.\N所以现在可以把5纳米、4纳米和3纳米更多地看作是有效晶体管密度的指示。
Dialogue: 0,1:06:20.38,1:06:26.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,How many transistors can I get per squared per square millimeter or per square micron.\N即每平方毫米或每平方微米能容纳多少个晶体管。
Dialogue: 0,1:06:26.28,1:06:31.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's more an indication of that instead of really the dimension.\N这更多是表明这一点，而不是真正的尺寸。
Dialogue: 0,1:06:31.06,1:06:32.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,Tony, anything you would like to add.\N安东尼，你还有什么想补充的吗？
Dialogue: 0,1:06:32.66,1:06:40.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah well, historically Ronald was right down to maybe 90 or 65 nanometers.\N是的，从历史上看，罗纳德在90纳米或65纳米左右是正确的。
Dialogue: 0,1:06:40.04,1:06:41.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's the half pitch.\N这是半间距。
Dialogue: 0,1:06:41.50,1:06:44.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,When you say 65, the half pitch is 65.\N当你说65纳米时，半间距就是65纳米。
Dialogue: 0,1:06:44.84,1:06:49.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,But nowadays, no, it's just a marketing name.\N但如今，不，这只是一个营销名称。
Dialogue: 0,1:06:49.72,1:06:51.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,But this is not recorded.\N但这段内容没有被记录下来。
Dialogue: 0,1:06:51.10,1:06:53.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,I don't want my customers to hear this.\N我不希望我的客户听到这些。
Dialogue: 0,1:06:53.96,1:06:54.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,They know.\N他们知道。
Dialogue: 0,1:06:54.92,1:06:59.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, it's so cut this part out of the recording, OK.\N是的，把这段从录音中剪掉，好吗？
Dialogue: 0,1:06:59.44,1:07:03.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,No, it is an improvement over the previous generation.\N不，这确实比前一代有所改进。
Dialogue: 0,1:07:03.13,1:07:07.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,But is it 2x the transistor density.\N但它的晶体管密度是前一代的2倍吗？
Dialogue: 0,1:07:07.69,1:07:10.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,No, those days are over.\N不，那些日子已经过去了。
Dialogue: 0,1:07:10.15,1:07:15.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,OK, so I'd like to ask a question about the optimization methods.\N好的，我想问一下关于优化方法的问题。
Dialogue: 0,1:07:15.83,1:07:24.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,So one of the great challenges in optimization is if you have a great many variables to optimize, it gets really quite hard.\N优化中的一个重大挑战是，如果你需要优化的变量非常多，就会变得非常困难。
Dialogue: 0,1:07:24.47,1:07:30.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now you spoke mostly about the source optimization.\N你现在主要讲的是光源优化。
Dialogue: 0,1:07:30.15,1:07:33.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,And there's a lot of variables in the source optimization.\N光源优化中有很多变量。
Dialogue: 0,1:07:33.59,1:07:39.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,But then you also mention the so-called OPC which is the mask optimization.\N但你同时也提到了所谓的 OPC，也就是掩模优化。
Dialogue: 0,1:07:39.11,1:07:46.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that adds even more variables because in principle the geometric shapes can be represented by an infinite number of variables.\N这会带来更多的变量，因为理论上几何形状可以用无限多个变量来表示。
Dialogue: 0,1:07:46.39,1:07:48.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,So you have a very huge number of variables.\N所以你面对的是一个极其庞大的变量数量。
Dialogue: 0,1:07:48.87,1:07:55.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,So shouldn't you be doing the optimization of all of them together rather than saying, I'm going to do source optimization.\N那你为什么不同时优化所有变量，而是说我要先做光源优化？
Dialogue: 0,1:07:55.49,1:08:01.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,You should be saying, I'm going to do the optimization of everything the source, the mask.\N你应该说，我要一次性优化所有内容，包括光源和掩模。
Dialogue: 0,1:08:01.11,1:08:03.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,I do it all in one big optimization.\N我把所有内容放在一个大的优化过程中完成。
Dialogue: 0,1:08:03.11,1:08:06.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah so let me first answer that question.\N好的，让我先回答这个问题。
Dialogue: 0,1:08:06.43,1:08:07.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,You're absolutely right.\N你说得完全正确。
Dialogue: 0,1:08:07.51,1:08:09.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,And perhaps I wasn't completely clear.\N或许我之前表达得还不够清楚。
Dialogue: 0,1:08:09.89,1:08:15.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,Remember that video where we picked some clips out of this huge design.\N记得我们从这个巨大设计中挑选了一些片段的那段视频吗？
Dialogue: 0,1:08:15.67,1:08:26.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,Those clips were supposed to be representative proxies for the incredible variety of patterns of the whole design.\N这些片段本应作为整个设计中惊人多样图案的代表性代理。
Dialogue: 0,1:08:26.85,1:08:33.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,When we optimize the illumination, we optimize that with the OPC for those clips.\N当我们优化照明时，是针对这些片段的 OPC 进行优化的。
Dialogue: 0,1:08:33.89,1:08:43.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then we but we cannot say, let's optimize the whole design with hundreds of billions of patterns,\N但我们不能说，让我们用数百亿个图案，
Dialogue: 0,1:08:43.09,1:08:49.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,together with all these variables in the illumination and with all of the variables in the projection optics.\N以及照明中的所有变量和投影光学中的所有变量，一起优化整个设计。
Dialogue: 0,1:08:49.65,1:08:53.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's simply computationally is not yet feasible.\N这在计算上目前还不可行。
Dialogue: 0,1:08:53.17,1:08:55.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,So what we do is we take some of these clips.\N所以我们做的，是选取其中一些片段。
Dialogue: 0,1:08:55.69,1:08:56.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,There we go.\N就是这样。
Dialogue: 0,1:08:56.51,1:09:05.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,Optimize then we say, now this illumination, probably since it is good for the most critical patterns, will be good for all of the other patterns.\N优化之后，我们说，既然这种照明对最关键的图案有效，那它很可能对其他所有图案也有效。
Dialogue: 0,1:09:05.74,1:09:09.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then we do the OPC, a divide and conquer strategy.\N然后我们再进行 OPC，采用分而治之的策略。
Dialogue: 0,1:09:09.06,1:09:12.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,Once the OPC stage, you don't change the design anymore.\NOPC 阶段之后，你就不能再修改设计了。
Dialogue: 0,1:09:12.94,1:09:17.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'm going to have to cut us off our no, no I'm sorry.\N我得打断一下，抱歉，不行。
Dialogue: 0,1:09:17.58,1:09:22.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,Our AV support is going to leave us and we're well after time.\N我们的自动驾驶支持团队要离开了，而且已经超时了。
Dialogue: 0,1:09:22.82,1:09:29.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,But the speakers will stick around and happy to answer questions one on one.\N但演讲者会留下来，很乐意一对一回答问题。
Dialogue: 0,1:09:29.68,1:09:33.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,So let's thank both speakers for a fascinating talk.\N那么，让我们感谢两位演讲者带来了一场精彩的分享。
Dialogue: 0,1:09:37.88,1:09:42.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,And if you want to stick around, I think they'll be glad to answer for the questions.\N如果你想留下来，我觉得他们很乐意继续回答问题。
Dialogue: 0,1:09:42.98,1:09:46.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,Absolutely, absolutely.\N当然，当然。
Dialogue: 0,1:09:46.80,1:09:48.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,How are you.\N你好啊。
Dialogue: 0,1:09:48.32,1:09:50.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,Oh, I haven't seen you for a long time.\N哦，好久没见你了。
Dialogue: 0,1:09:50.70,1:09:53.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah so I'm doing fine.\N是的，我一切都好。
Dialogue: 0,1:09:53.02,1:09:56.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I understand that you are retired or not yet.\N我听说你已经退休了，还是还没退休？
Dialogue: 0,1:09:56.86,1:09:58.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yes, I am semi-retired.\N是的，我半退休了。
Dialogue: 0,1:09:58.42,1:09:59.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'm\N我
